; pi4NeonLiar.pi4 - STAGES 0, 0.5 and 0.6 of the NeonArcade port.
;
;   STAGE 0    one real Neon chrome screen, drawn with NO new library:
;              today's unmodified display.pi4, DisplayFillRect for
;              Neon_Box with the alpha thrown away, DisplayCharClear
;              with the built-in 8x16 font for Neon_Text, and the
;              palette converted by hand into DisplayRGB calls.
;
;   STAGE 0.5  four timed passes over the whole 32bpp surface, so that
;              the compositor in stage 1 is designed against a measured
;              fill rate and a measured blend rate rather than a guess.
;
;   STAGE 0.6  the same screen again, three times, each with ONE widget
;              different and only the damage repainted - and a pixel-by-
;              pixel proof that the fast frame is the same picture as
;              the slow one. Added 2026-08-26 with
;              RaspberryPi4/Lib/damage.pi4.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship. It changes nothing that does: display.pi4
; is not touched, and the one file it adds under Lib/ - damage.pi4 -
; includes nothing and drives no hardware.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4\Examples\Diagnostics\NotBuilding\pi4NeonLiar.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o _work\liar.img
;
; ======================================================================
; THE TWO NAMED LIES. READ THESE BEFORE LOOKING AT THE SCREEN.
; ======================================================================
; The plan calls this diagnostic "the liar" and the name is the point.
; Two things on the panel will be visibly wrong. BOTH ARE INTENDED, both
; are consequences of using display.pi4 unmodified, and NEITHER IS A
; DEFECT. Do not report them.
;
;   LIE 1 - EVERY GLOW AND EVERY HOVER IS FULLY OPAQUE.
;
;     Neon_Glow (GlUIChrome.pbi:323-328) is four nested boxes at alpha
;     0.10/1.5, /3, /4.5, /6 - so between 2% and 7% opacity each. It is
;     meant to read as a faint lift. display.pi4 has no blend anywhere
;     in it - DisplayFillRect is an opaque run fill (display.pi4:2414-2455)
;     and DisplayBlit is a straight copy (display.pi4:2668-2682) - so
;     every one of those four boxes lands at FULL strength.
;
;     WHAT YOU WILL SEE: a hard four-pixel cyan halo around the hovered
;     button instead of a soft glow, and a hard four-pixel BLACK border
;     around the open drop-down where its 45% shadow should be
;     (GlUIChrome.pbi:772). The hovered list row and the hovered menu
;     row are solid instead of faded. That is the missing alpha channel
;     made visible, which is exactly what stage 1 exists to add.
;
;   LIE 2 - EVERY GLYPH IS THE WRONG WIDTH.
;
;     The engine's text is proportional, antialiased, from a rasterised
;     atlas (GlUtilities.pbi:1191-1193). display.pi4's font is 8 wide by
;     16 tall, ASCII 32..126, one bit per pixel, FIXED WIDTH
;     (display.pi4:530-538).
;
;     WHAT YOU WILL SEE: every string is exactly 8 pixels per character.
;     Menu tabs are narrower than they should be, "I" is as wide as "W",
;     the right-aligned shortcut column does not line up with anything,
;     and there is no antialiasing at all. Stage 2 is what fixes this,
;     and section 4.4 of the plan says how.
;
; THERE IS A THIRD WRONGNESS AND IT IS NOT THIS PROGRAM'S.
;
;     Anvil prints two or three lines of its own after a payload returns
;     - "## deadman stopped", "## returned, x0 = ..." and then the
;     prompt (RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4:1497, :1512). When the
;     screen is up those lines go through the UART mirror onto the
;     console rectangle, which is most of the panel. So a short band of
;     Anvil's own text WILL be stamped across the picture as soon as
;     this program returns.
;
;     That is Anvil, not the diagnostic, and there is nothing this
;     payload can do about it: Anvil's console cursor lives in Anvil's
;     copy of display.pi4's globals and is not reachable from here.
;     Bring the screen up FRESH (reset, then "s") before running this,
;     so Anvil's cursor is near the TOP of its console and only a few
;     rows are lost. On a console that has already scrolled to the
;     bottom, those closing lines will SCROLL THE WHOLE PICTURE UP by
;     one text row each, which is ugly but still recognisable.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT STAGE 0 IS ACTUALLY PROVING
; ======================================================================
; Section 1.1 of the plan makes one claim and the whole port rests on
; it: that the entire 1367-line application chrome draws through exactly
; TWO primitives plus a scissor.
;
;   Neon_Box   GlUIChrome.pbi:302-304   -> DisplayFillRect here
;   Neon_Text  GlUIChrome.pbi:306-308   -> DisplayCharClear here
;   Neon_Scissor* GlUIChrome.pbi:404-422 -> NlClipSet/NlClipClear here
;
; Everything else in that file is composed from those three. Neon_Frame
; is four boxes (:330-335). Neon_Glow is four boxes (:323-328).
; Neon_Panel is five boxes and a text (:339-348). The menu check mark is
; seven boxes (:879-885). This program transcribes those compositions
; literally, and it COUNTS the primitive calls it made and prints the
; totals. If the count comes back as fills and glyphs and nothing else,
; and a recognisable menu bar, panel and list appear, the claim holds.
;
; THE SCISSOR IS THE ONE PLACE THIS PROGRAM ADDS CODE display.pi4 DOES
; NOT HAVE, and it is worth being exact about what it does and does not
; do. display.pi4 clips every primitive to the SURFACE (its own header
; says so, at :2354-2357) and has no notion of a caller's clip
; rectangle. So NlBox intersects the rectangle with the clip before
; calling DisplayFillRect - a few lines of arithmetic, no new library -
; and NlText drops any GLYPH CELL that is not wholly inside the clip.
;
;   THAT MAKES THE TEXT CLIP GLYPH-GRANULAR, NOT PIXEL-EXACT. A row of
;   list text half way over the bottom edge of its box disappears
;   entirely instead of being cut in half. That is a stage 0 limitation
;   and it is named here so nobody mistakes it for a third lie:
;   GfxClipSet in stage 1 is pixel-exact by construction, because it
;   clips runs and not cells.
;
; The list in the picture is deliberately scrolled, and deliberately has
; more rows than fit, so the clip has something to do. The counters at
; the end print how many boxes were narrowed and how many glyphs were
; dropped. If those are zero, the clip proved nothing and the screen is
; lying about a third thing.
;
; NO FLOATING POINT ANYWHERE. Section 3.1 of the plan: .f arithmetic is
; refused outright by this backend (EmitCore_A64.pbi:543-544) and
; software binary32 costs 1751 instructions per multiply
; (math.pi4:114-125). Every coordinate here is a .i, every palette
; entry is an integer 0..255 converted by hand from GlUIChrome.pbi's
; floats, and Neon_UIScale is 1.0 so Neon_S() is the identity and does
; not appear. This is also the first small test of the .f -> .i
; conversion the plan schedules as stage 3 across 460 sites.
;
; ======================================================================
; STAGE 0.5 - WHAT IS MEASURED, AND THE DECISION IT DRIVES
; ======================================================================
; The plan's stage 0.5 asks for three numbers. THE FIRST IS ALREADY
; ANSWERED and is not re-measured here:
;
;   SCTLR_EL2 = $30C50830, at EL2, measured on this board on 2026-08-26
;   by RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4MmuState.pi4. That is
;   exactly what Datasheets/pi4/armstub8.S:136-141 writes, and every set
;   bit in it is RES1. THE MMU IS OFF, THE D-CACHE IS OFF, THE I-CACHE
;   IS OFF. Measured, not assumed.
;
;   Note in passing that display.pi4:426-429 and xhci.pi4:275 still say
;   the opposite. They are STALE - inherited from the U-Boot era, when
;   an image entered through "go" ran on U-Boot's page tables. Anvil
;   boots directly now. Those two comments should be corrected in place
;   by whoever owns those files; this diagnostic does not touch them.
;
; So every access below is Device memory: no caching, no gathering, no
; write combining. That is the configuration the numbers describe, and
; it is why they are worth taking. MMU stage 1 has since been proven on
; this board (see THE DECISION below), but nothing has turned it on for
; this payload: the register above is still what a run of this program
; will find, and these are therefore MMU-OFF numbers. Say so beside them
; whenever they are quoted.
;
; FOUR PASSES, not two, because two numbers cannot separate the two
; possible walls:
;
;   1. CLEAR   DisplayClear() through the existing unrolled wide-store
;              loop (display.pi4:2414-2455). Pure opaque stores. This is
;              the fill-rate ceiling, and its header claims about 1.4
;              instructions per byte at :2386-2389, which this pass is
;              in a position to check.
;
;   2. READ    every pixel read and accumulated, nothing written. This
;              number is not in the plan and it earns its place: it is
;              the only way to tell a read-bound wall from a write-bound
;              one, and the compositor's blend does both.
;
;   3. BLEND, PLAIN   read, unpack three channels into locals, blend,
;              repack, store. This is what stage 1's GfxFill blended
;              path looks like when it is written to be read.
;
;   4. BLEND, FUSED   the same arithmetic with the per-channel locals
;              eliminated, which is what DspRowFill32's own header
;              (display.pi4:2372-2384) says you have to do on this
;              backend: procedure locals are STATICALLY ALLOCATED, so
;              every mention of one costs an adrp/add/ldr or
;              adrp/add/str, and a one-pixel body that stores one pixel
;              was measured at THIRTY instructions.
;
; PASSES 3 AND 4 ARE THE FINDING, TOGETHER. If they are close, the wall
; is memory and the MMU is the answer. If 4 is far better than 3, a
; large part of the cost is the calling convention rather than the
; DRAM, and the first fix is codegen discipline in the compositor's
; inner loop, not page tables. One number could not have told them
; apart.
;
; THE DECISION, AND IT GOT CHEAPER TO ACT ON WHILE THIS WAS BEING
; WRITTEN. Per the plan's stage 0.5: if the full-surface blend pass is
; worse than about 25 ms, a 1920x1080 alpha-composited UI is not
; reachable in this memory configuration and the MMU work moves ahead
; of everything else.
;
;   WHEN THE PLAN WAS WRITTEN that was an expensive verdict, because
;   RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4 was unproven code waiting on three
;   assembler encodings and turning it on was a research project.
;
;   IT IS NOT ANY MORE. MMU stage 1 PASSED ON THIS BOARD on 2026-08-26,
;   after this file was started: SCTLR_EL2 went $30C50830 -> $30C50831
;   -> $30C50830, with the console alive the whole way through, and
;   both the PCIe window above 4 GiB and the ARM-local window mapped
;   correctly. So "the MMU moves ahead of everything else" now means
;   enabling something that has been switched on and off once already
;   on real silicon, not opening a new front.
;
;   WHICH CHANGES HOW TO READ AN OVER-BUDGET NUMBER. It is no longer a
;   reason to redesign the compositor around dirty rectangles before
;   trying anything else. Mapping DRAM as Normal Cacheable and
;   re-running these same four passes is now the cheapest experiment
;   available, and it should be the next thing done - the numbers below
;   are the before half of a before-and-after that can be had in an
;   afternoon.
;
; This program computes the verdict on the board and prints it, so
; nobody has to divide two numbers at the end of a long session.
;
; EACH PASS IS PROBED BEFORE IT IS RUN. Eight rows are timed first, the
; full-surface cost extrapolated from them, and the estimate PRINTED.
; The full pass then runs only if the estimate is under five seconds.
; Nothing on this disk measures uncached store bandwidth on this board,
; so "how long will four full-surface passes take" is genuinely unknown
; before the first one, and a diagnostic that hangs into the deadman
; having printed nothing is worth nothing. Every pass drains the UART
; the moment it has a number, so a run that dies part way through is
; still informative - the house style pi4DisplayProbe.pi4 sets with its
; numbered sections.
;
; THE ARM CLOCK IS ASKED FOR, NOT ASSUMED. The part boots at 600 MHz and
; reaches 1500 MHz only when something raises it. Every cycles-per-byte
; figure below is computed against the rate the firmware reports through
; MailboxClockRateMeasured(3), and that rate is printed beside them, so
; an instructions-per-byte claim cannot silently inherit the wrong
; clock.
;
; AUTO-FLUSH IS OFF FOR THE TIMED PASSES AND FOR THE PICTURE, and it is
; measured separately as a fifth number. display.pi4 documents that
; escape itself (:463-464): draw a lot with it off, DisplayFlush() once
; at the end. With the MMU off the dc cvac is architecturally a no-op,
; but it still executes 129,600 times over a 1824x984 surface, and
; knowing what that costs is what decides whether stage 1's GfxFlushRect
; can stay on by default.
;
; ======================================================================
; STAGE 0.6 - DIRTY RECTANGLES, AND WHY THEY ARE THE ANSWER HERE
; ======================================================================
; Stage 0.5 came back over budget by two orders of magnitude and the
; conclusion drawn from it at the time was "turn the MMU on". That was
; done, and it bought 1.3x, not the 100x that was needed:
;
;                         MMU off      MMU on, DRAM cacheable
;   clear, stores only    356.9 ms          266.4 ms
;   read, loads only     1787.0 ms         1155.0 ms
;   blend, best of two   2718.8 ms         2172.0 ms   vs a 25 ms budget
;   this Neon screen     1184.7 ms          922.5 ms
;
; THE CACHES COULD NOT HAVE HELPED MORE THAN THAT and it is worth being
; clear about why, because "add cache" is the reflex. A compositor
; STREAMS: every cache line is touched once per frame and then never
; again, so there is no reuse for a cache to find. All it can
; contribute is burst fills, and 1.3x is about what burst fills are
; worth.
;
; So there are two independent problems and stage 0.6 solves exactly
; one of them.
;
;   PROBLEM ONE - A PIXEL COSTS FAR TOO MUCH. 266.4 ms of pure opaque
;   stores over 1,794,816 pixels is 148 ns each, which at 1500 MHz is
;   222 CYCLES TO WRITE FOUR BYTES, through a loop whose own header
;   claims about 1.4 instructions per byte (display.pi4:2386-2389). One
;   of those two numbers is wrong and this program cannot tell which -
;   it would take a cycle-accurate look at the emitted loop, which
;   belongs to whoever owns the code generator. IT IS NOT A MEMORY
;   BANDWIDTH STORY: 8.29 MB in 266 ms is 31 MB/s, and this part's DRAM
;   does not run at 31 MB/s.
;
;   PROBLEM TWO - THE PROGRAM TOUCHES 8 MB EVERY FRAME TO CHANGE A
;   BUTTON. That one needs no compiler work at all, and it is this one.
;
; A UI THAT HOVERS A BUTTON SHOULD REPAINT A BUTTON. Sections 9a to 9d
; below draw this same screen with one widget different and repaint
; only what changed, and print both numbers so the ratio is on the
; serial line rather than in a claim.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RECTANGLES, NOT TILES, AND THE REASON IS THE 148 ns
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; The full argument with its arithmetic is in RaspberryPi4/Lib/
; damage.pi4's header. The short form:
;
;   A TILE GRID is easier - one bit per tile, OR them together, repaint
;   the marked ones, no coalescing to get wrong. Its cost is the edge
;   pixels of a widget that does not fill its tiles, and on this
;   hardware an edge pixel costs the same 148 ns as a useful one. A
;   caret is 2 x 16; at 64-pixel tiles it repaints up to 8192 pixels to
;   change 32, which is a 256x tax on the commonest event in any UI.
;
;   MAKE THE TILES SMALL ENOUGH TO FIX THAT and the bitmap becomes
;   28,000 tiles, and walking it every frame is O(SURFACE) - which is
;   the exact thing damage tracking was bought to stop paying. The two
;   costs move in opposite directions and they cross ABOVE the cost of
;   doing it exactly.
;
;   SO: EXACT RECTANGLES, with a merge rule derived from what a
;   rectangle actually costs. Merging two of them wastes the pixels in
;   their union that were in neither; keeping them apart costs the
;   repaint driver one more intersection test per primitive, about
;   27 us across a thousand-primitive screen. 27 us at 148 ns a pixel
;   is 182 pixels, so the threshold is 256 and it is settable.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT SECTION 9 PRINTS, AND HOW TO READ IT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   9a  A CARET BLINK. One rectangle, 32 pixels. This is the floor, and
;       what it measures is NOT the pixels - it is the cost of walking
;       the widget tree and asking the damage list about every
;       primitive in it while painting almost nothing. If 9a is a large
;       fraction of the frame budget, the next thing to build is a
;       retained widget tree, not a better damage rule. That is the
;       most useful single number in this section.
;
;   9b  A HOVER MOVING BETWEEN TWO BUTTONS. TWO rectangles, and it must
;       stay two - the two buttons are 500 pixels apart and their union
;       wastes about 13,000 pixels, which is 1.9 ms to save 27 us. If
;       this prints one rectangle, the slack is set too high.
;       BOTH BUTTONS ARE DAMAGED, the one losing the glow as well as
;       the one gaining it, and each is damaged FOUR PIXELS OVERSIZE
;       because Neon_Glow's ring is four nested boxes outside the
;       button (GlUIChrome.pbi:323-328). Damage the button only and the
;       old halo stays on screen as a hollow cyan frame around nothing.
;
;   9c  A LIST SCROLLED ONE ROW. The whole well, because every row in
;       it moved. THIS IS THE WORST CASE AND IT IS REPORTED AS SUCH -
;       dirty rectangles are not uniformly a hundredfold win, and
;       anyone quoting the caret number without this one is quoting
;       half the picture. (A hardware blitter turns this into a copy
;       plus one row; that is the DMA 2D engine in section 9.3 of the
;       port plan and it is deliberately not done here.)
;
;   9d  ALL THREE AT ONCE. The busiest frame a real UI has. It also
;       prints what the BOUNDING BOX of that damage would have been, so
;       the value of having a cost model rather than just taking the
;       union is visible as a number.
;
;   9e  THE IDENTITY CHECK, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST.
;       A fast frame that is subtly wrong is worse than a slow one. So
;       the program draws state B in full and keeps the surface, draws
;       state A in full over it, applies the event that turns A into B
;       with only its damage repainted, and then COMPARES EVERY PIXEL.
;       Zero differences means the fast path and the slow path produce
;       the same picture. Anything else means an event under-reported
;       its damage, and the timings above are the cost of drawing the
;       wrong thing quickly.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SECTION 10 - THE SHADOW-BUFFER QUESTION, SETTLED BY ONE PAIR
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; With the MMU on and DRAM cacheable a shadow buffer is viable where it
; was not before, so the question is live. It reduces to one thing:
; DOES A STORE INTO THE FRAMEBUFFER COST WHAT THE IDENTICAL STORE INTO
; ORDINARY DRAM COSTS? Section 10 runs the same unrolled loop against
; both and prints the ratio and a verdict.
;
; If they are the same, a shadow can only add work - write the pixel
; twice, read it once more - and damage-repaint straight into the live
; framebuffer is right. If the framebuffer is much slower, then the
; framebuffer is the wall, and composing in DRAM while copying out only
; the DAMAGED rectangles is the answer - in which case the shadow
; buffer and the damage layer are one decision, because it is the
; damage that makes the copy small.
;
; TEARING IS NOT WHAT DECIDES IT, at these rates. A caret repaint
; measured in microseconds cannot be caught half-drawn by a 60 Hz
; scan-out in any way a person could see. A full repaint at hundreds of
; milliseconds tears with or without a shadow buffer. Tearing becomes a
; real question only when a frame is both large and fast, and this
; hardware is not there.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT STAGE 0.6 DID NOT TOUCH
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; display.pi4. Not one line. The damage layer needs nothing from it
; that it does not already export - DisplayWidth/Height for the bind,
; DisplayFillRect to paint, DisplayFlushRect to make a rectangle
; visible - and adding a damage hook to the file that drives Anvil's
; own console would have been risk taken for no gain.
;
; It also does not touch a DMA engine, and that is the point of putting
; the tracking in its own file: damage.pi4 says WHICH pixels, and has
; no opinion about who moves them. When the accelerated blit arrives it
; goes underneath this unchanged.
;
; ======================================================================
; THE BENCH PROCEDURE
; ======================================================================
;   pmf> r                      reset, so Anvil's console starts empty
;   pmf> s                      screen on - REQUIRED, see below
;   pmf> deadman 30
;   pmf> map              <- it prints `stage a file at <stage>`. The
;                            --load-addr above does not pin the image.
;   pmf> receive <stage> <len> <crc>
;   pmf> run <stage>
;
; THE SCREEN MUST BE UP BEFORE THE RUN, and not because this payload
; needs it - it brings up its own framebuffer. It is because Anvil's
; screenshot command refuses when gScreen is 0 (anvil.pi4:3318-3321),
; so without "s" first there is no way to get the picture off the board.
;
; DEADMAN 30 RATHER THAN THE HOUSE 15, and here is the reason rather
; than the exception. Four full-surface passes over 8.29 MB of memory
; whose speed nothing on this disk has ever measured is the one payload
; in this directory whose runtime genuinely cannot be predicted before
; it is run once. The five-second-per-pass estimate gate above bounds it
; at roughly twenty seconds in the worst case it will attempt.
;
; STAGE 0.6 PUSHED THAT UP AGAIN AND 30 IS NOW TIGHT. Sections 8 to 10
; add, on the cached numbers: four full repaints at about 0.9 s each
; (one for the picture, two for the identity check's two reference
; states, one to leave the panel correct), a whole-surface copy, and a
; whole-surface compare - the compare is a read pass, which stage 0.5
; measured at 1.16 s. Call it six seconds on top of the eleven the
; stage 0.5 passes take.
;
;   USE `deadman 45` UNTIL THE FIRST FULL RUN HAS PRINTED ITS OWN
;   TOTALS, then tighten it to whatever that run actually took plus a
;   third. The BCM2711 counter is 20 bits at 65536/s, so ~15.999 s is
;   the ceiling a SINGLE arming can express (safety.pi4:491-498 clamps
;   and returns the real value); Anvil's `deadman` command re-arms, so
;   the number typed at the prompt is not bounded the same way. If a
;   run does reset the board part way through, the sections above the
;   reset have already printed - every one of them drains the UART the
;   moment it has a number, for exactly this case.
;
; Afterwards, for the picture:
;   python tools/pmfshot.py COM7 liar.png
;
; WITHOUT A BOARD, and this is new: the whole program can be run on the
; project's A64 oracle against a fabricated VideoCore, at 800 x 600,
; with the MMU switch forced off -
;
;   python tools/a64/a64_damage_check.py --only-neon
;
; Its TIMINGS ARE FICTION there (time is a step counter) but section
; 9e's pixel-by-pixel identity check is real arithmetic and is checked
; by the harness. Use it to know the frames are CORRECT before spending
; bench time finding out how FAST they are.
;
; ======================================================================
; WHAT YOU SHOULD SEE ON THE HDMI PANEL
; ======================================================================
; A dark grey-blue application window - NOT black. The ground is
; (33,34,37), which is GlUIChrome.pbi:57's 0.129/0.133/0.145 converted,
; and its header says why it is grey rather than black at :47-49.
;
; From the top:
;
;   a MENU BAR across the full width, one shade lighter than the ground,
;   with a hairline under it and five root labels - File, Edit, Board,
;   Tools, Help. BOARD IS DRAWN OPEN: its tab is cyan-tinted with a
;   two-pixel cyan underline;
;
;   its DROP-DOWN hanging below it, with the hard black four-pixel
;   border that is LIE 1, a cyan-ish hairline frame, six rows, one
;   separator hairline, one row hovered in solid cyan-grey, one row
;   showing the seven-box green CHECK MARK, one row greyed out as
;   disabled, and right-aligned shortcut text that does not line up
;   because of LIE 2;
;
;   a TAB STRIP under the menu bar with three tabs, the middle one
;   active - lighter, with a two-pixel cyan indicator under it;
;
;   a LEFT PANEL titled PROJECT with a cyan tab down its left edge,
;   containing a scrolling LIST - a sixteen-row well holding a
;   twenty-eight-row tree, scrolled six rows down. So its first visible
;   row is not its first row and it overflows at BOTH edges, which is
;   what gives the clip something to do.
;
;     Look at the top and bottom edges of that well specifically. There
;     is a partly-visible hovered row at each - and what you will see of
;     it is a SLIVER OF A BACKGROUND BAR WITH NO TEXT IN IT. That is the
;     two clips behaving differently on purpose: the box is cut to the
;     pixel, and the glyph cell is dropped whole because a 1bpp
;     fixed-cell renderer cannot draw half a character. It is named in
;     the scissor note above and it is a stage 0 limitation, not a
;     third lie - stage 1's GfxClipSet is pixel-exact on both.
;
;     Inside the well: one row selected (mmu.pi4 - a cyan bar down its
;     left edge and cyan text), several amber group headings at
;     different indent depths, right-aligned faint file sizes, and a
;     scrollbar thumb about sixteen twenty-eighths of the track high
;     sitting a fifth of the way down it;
;
;   BELOW THE LIST, in the same panel, a hairline-framed DETAIL STRIP
;   with three lines in it. It is there because the list well is
;   deliberately too small and something honest had to occupy the space
;   the list gave up;
;
;   a RIGHT PANEL titled SERIAL MONITOR with a few lines of text, a
;   TEXT FIELD reading COM7 115200 with a cyan frame and a two-pixel
;   cyan CARET after the text, and below it a row of four BUTTONS - one
;   normal, one lit cyan, one disabled and faint, and one HOVERED,
;   which is the one wearing the hard cyan halo of LIE 1;
;
;     THE CARET AND THE HOVER ARE THE STAGE 0.6 SUBJECTS. The caret is
;     the smallest thing on the panel - two pixels by sixteen - and the
;     hovered button with its four-pixel ring is the largest thing a
;     pointer changes. Section 9 repaints each of them alone and times
;     it. By the time the run ends the caret has blinked twice and the
;     hover has been moved and put back, so what is on the panel at the
;     end is the picture described here;
;
;   a STATUS BAR across the bottom, with the two lies restated on it in
;   text so that anyone photographing the screen has the caveat in the
;   photograph.
;
; AND ONE THING YOU SHOULD NOT SEE: any curve, any gradient, any soft
; edge, any anti-aliased glyph. Every pixel on that panel came out of
; exactly two calls, DisplayFillRect and DisplayCharClear, and the run
; prints how many of each it made. If the picture looks like an
; application anyway, section 1.1 of the plan is right and the port is
; tractable. That is the whole of what stage 0 is for.
;
; WHAT WOULD TELL YOU IT WENT WRONG, as opposed to lied:
;
;   * nothing on the panel at all, and no UART output - the payload
;     never got control, or the UART is not where uart.pi4 thinks it is.
;     Not a graphics finding.
;   * UART output but a black screen - DisplayInit failed. The code and
;     DisplayLastError() are printed; -1 is #DSP_ENOTAGS, -5 is the
;     set-physical tag, and display.pi4:916-957 has the rest.
;   * A PICTURE THAT LEANS DIAGONALLY - the pitch is being used as if it
;     were width*bpp somewhere. It is not used at all in this file:
;     every pixel here goes through display.pi4's primitives except the
;     three timed passes, which take DisplayPitch() explicitly. If this
;     appears, it is in display.pi4 and it is a real bug in a file that
;     has been proven on silicon, so say so loudly.
;   * red and blue swapped - the firmware's pixel order disagrees with
;     display.pi4's packing. This program asks the firmware, exactly as
;     anvil.pi4:3211-3215 does, and prints the answer.
;   * the screenshot showing Anvil's console rather than this picture -
;     the second DisplayInit moved the framebuffer. This program prints
;     the base address it was given; compare it with what Anvil reports.
;     That would be a real finding about re-initialising a framebuffer
;     that is already allocated, and it is worth writing down.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
; #DSP_DMA_LINKED - MANDATORY, and it must be defined BEFORE display.pi4.
; 0 = the CPU drawing path and nothing else; every procedure that would
; touch the blitter is skipped at the token level and never parsed, and
; what display.pi4 still carries is ten accessors totalling 660 bytes so
; that a program can ask whether the engine was linked and be told no.
; 1 would additionally require RaspberryPi4/Lib/dma.pi4 to be included
; ahead of display.pi4.
; display.pi4's THE DMA SEAM section has the reasoning; there is no
; default because a default in the library and a different value here
; would be a hard "Constant already defined" error.
#DSP_DMA_LINKED = 0
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/display.pi4"

; mmu.pi4, so this program can measure the SAME passes with the caches on
; as it measured with them off. Stage 0.5's whole verdict was that a
; composited UI is unreachable in an uncached memory configuration; the
; only honest way to test that claim is to change the configuration and
; re-run the identical code.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4"

; damage.pi4, so this program can draw the SAME screen twice - once in
; full, once repainting only what a single widget changed - and time
; both with the same clock, through the same primitives, in the same
; run. Two numbers from two programs would not be comparable.
;
; It includes nothing, touches no hardware, and is proven separately by
; RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/NotBuilding/pi4DamageSelfTest.pi4 and
; tools/a64/a64_damage_check.py. See STAGE 0.6 in the header.
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/damage.pi4"


; ======================================================================
;  OUTPUT HELPERS
;
;  No character literals anywhere - house rule. The ASCII codes used
;  below are 32 space, 36 dollar, 46 full stop, 48 the digit zero, 55
;  the letter A minus ten, and 58 colon. Each is named where it is used.
; ======================================================================

Procedure PutHexN(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)                      ; 48 is the digit zero
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)                      ; 55 is the letter A minus ten
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

; PutFix2 - a value that has already been multiplied by 100, printed as
; a decimal with two places. Integer arithmetic throughout: .f is
; refused by this backend and a software float costs 1751 instructions
; per multiply (math.pi4:114-125), which is not a price a diagnostic
; should pay to print "1.40".
Procedure PutFix2(v100.i)
  Define w.i
  Define f.i
  w = v100 / 100
  f = v100 - (w * 100)
  If f < 0
    f = 0 - f
  EndIf
  PrintDec(w)
  UartWrite(46)                              ; 46 is the full stop
  UartWrite(48 + (f / 10))
  UartWrite(48 + (f - ((f / 10) * 10)))
EndProcedure

; PutMs - microseconds printed as milliseconds with three places, which
; is the unit the 25 ms decision is stated in.
Procedure PutMs(us.i)
  Define w.i
  Define f.i
  w = us / 1000
  f = us - (w * 1000)
  PrintDec(w)
  UartWrite(46)                              ; 46 is the full stop
  UartWrite(48 + (f / 100))
  UartWrite(48 + ((f / 10) - ((f / 100) * 10)))
  UartWrite(48 + (f - ((f / 10) * 10)))
EndProcedure

Procedure PutLabel(*s)
  UartWriteStr("    ")
  UartWriteStr(*s)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE PALETTE, CONVERTED BY HAND
; ======================================================================
;  GlUIChrome.pbi:57-68 holds twelve colours as .f triples in 0..1. Each
;  one below is that triple times 255, rounded to nearest, and the
;  rounding is checked against the one place the engine states the
;  answer itself: its palette header says the ground is "(33,34,37)"
;  (GlUIChrome.pbi:48), and 0.129/0.133/0.145 times 255 is
;  32.9/33.9/37.0. They agree, so the conversion below is the same
;  conversion the engine's author did.
;
;  These are DERIVED colours as well as the twelve, because the widgets
;  multiply the palette rather than naming new entries - an open menu
;  tab is accent times 0.30/0.30/0.34 (GlUIChrome.pbi:725), a drop-down
;  frame is accent times 0.55 (:774), a scrollbar thumb under the
;  pointer is accent times 0.8 (:485). Those products are done here, at
;  author time, in the same by-hand way, so that the drawing code below
;  is a transcription and not an arithmetic exercise.
;
;  THEY ARE COMPUTED AT RUN TIME, NOT AS CONSTANTS, because DisplayRGB
;  packs against the surface's depth and against DisplayColourSwap
;  (display.pi4:2172-2192). A packed constant would be a 32bpp
;  no-swap assumption baked into twelve places.
; ======================================================================

; One declaration per line - this dialect takes one name per Global.
Global cBg.i
Global cPanel.i
Global cHead.i
Global cLine.i
Global cText.i
Global cDim.i
Global cFaint.i
Global cAcc.i
Global cAmb.i
Global cGrn.i
Global cRed.i
Global cVio.i

Global cAccTab.i          ; accent * 0.30 0.30 0.34 - open menu tab, selected row
Global cAccRow.i          ; accent * 0.28 0.28 0.32 - hovered menu row
Global cAccFrame.i        ; accent * 0.55         - drop-down frame
Global cAccThumb.i        ; accent * 0.8          - scrollbar thumb, hot
Global cThumb.i           ; 0.31 0.33 0.36        - scrollbar thumb, idle
Global cBtnLit.i          ; 0.157 0.365 0.408     - a lit button's face
Global cBtnLitText.i      ; 0.588 0.816 0.867     - a lit button's label
Global cBtnTop.i          ; 0.216 0.259 0.325     - a button's top highlight
Global cListBg.i          ; 0.118 0.122 0.133     - a list's well
Global cBlack.i           ; the drop-down's shadow, which has no alpha here

Procedure NlPalette()
  ; The twelve, GlUIChrome.pbi:57-68, in file order.
  cBg    = DisplayRGB( 33,  34,  37)
  cPanel = DisplayRGB( 37,  38,  41)
  cHead  = DisplayRGB( 43,  44,  47)
  cLine  = DisplayRGB( 50,  51,  54)
  cText  = DisplayRGB(220, 220, 220)
  cDim   = DisplayRGB(147, 152, 162)
  cFaint = DisplayRGB(111, 117, 127)
  cAcc   = DisplayRGB( 90, 189, 208)
  cAmb   = DisplayRGB(225, 165,  66)
  cGrn   = DisplayRGB( 92, 191, 152)
  cRed   = DisplayRGB(221, 126, 126)
  cVio   = DisplayRGB(169, 152, 224)

  ; The derived ones. Each is the accent triple above scaled by the
  ; factor the widget uses, rounded to nearest.
  cAccTab   = DisplayRGB( 27,  57,  71)      ; :725  0.30 0.30 0.34
  cAccRow   = DisplayRGB( 25,  53,  67)      ; :856  0.28 0.28 0.32
  cAccFrame = DisplayRGB( 50, 104, 114)      ; :774  0.55
  cAccThumb = DisplayRGB( 72, 151, 166)      ; :485  0.80
  cThumb    = DisplayRGB( 79,  84,  92)      ; :484  0.31 0.33 0.36
  cBtnLit     = DisplayRGB( 40,  93, 104)    ; :358
  cBtnLitText = DisplayRGB(150, 208, 221)    ; :359
  cBtnTop     = DisplayRGB( 55,  66,  83)    ; :369
  cListBg     = DisplayRGB( 30,  31,  34)    ; :1307
  cBlack      = DisplayRGB(  0,   0,   0)    ; :772, the shadow
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE THREE PRIMITIVES
;
;  Neon_Box, Neon_Text and the scissor, and nothing else. Every widget
;  below is built out of these, in the same compositions GlUIChrome.pbi
;  uses, because that is the claim being tested.
; ======================================================================

Global nlClipOn.i = 0
Global nlClipX.i
Global nlClipY.i
Global nlClipW.i
Global nlClipH.i

Global nlFills.i = 0        ; DisplayFillRect calls made
Global nlGlyphs.i = 0       ; DisplayCharClear calls made
Global nlTexts.i = 0        ; DisplayTextClear calls made
Global nlNarrowed.i = 0     ; boxes the clip cut down
Global nlDropped.i = 0      ; boxes the clip removed entirely
Global nlGlyphsCut.i = 0    ; glyph cells the clip removed entirely
Global nlPixels.i = 0       ; pixels actually handed to DisplayFillRect

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE DAMAGE SWITCH - STAGE 0.6.
;
; nlDmgOn = 0 is the stage 0 behaviour and nothing above changes: every
; primitive draws, and drawing the screen costs the whole screen.
;
; nlDmgOn = 1 makes the two primitives consult damage.pi4 before they
; draw anything. The widget tree is walked EXACTLY ONCE either way -
; the alternative, re-running the tree once per damage rectangle, pays
; for the layout arithmetic N times and damage.pi4's header sets out
; why that is the wrong way round.
;
; THE CLIP AND THE DAMAGE ARE TWO DIFFERENT THINGS AND BOTH APPLY.
; NlClipSet is the widget's own scissor - the list well saying "nothing
; of mine may paint outside this box". The damage list is the frame
; saying "nothing at all may paint outside these boxes". A primitive
; has to survive both, in that order, and the order matters only for
; the counters: a box the scissor already removed is not a box the
; damage layer saved.
Global nlDmgOn.i = 0
Global nlDmgSkipped.i = 0   ; primitives the damage list rejected outright
Global nlDmgSplit.i = 0     ; primitives drawn once per damage rect, > once

; Neon_ScissorSet, GlUIChrome.pbi:404-418, with both of its coordinate
; flips gone: there is no GL scissor box counting up from the bottom of
; a DPI-scaled drawable here, there is a rectangle in surface pixels and
; the surface is the canvas. That simplification is the whole of section
; 6.3 of the plan in one procedure.
Procedure NlClipSet(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i)
  nlClipOn = 1
  nlClipX = x
  nlClipY = y
  nlClipW = w
  nlClipH = h
EndProcedure

Procedure NlClipClear()
  nlClipOn = 0
EndProcedure

; Neon_Box, GlUIChrome.pbi:302-304. The alpha argument of the original
; is simply not here: there is nothing that could be done with it, and a
; parameter that is accepted and ignored is worse than one that is
; absent, because it reads as though it works. LIE 1 lives in this
; absence.
Procedure NlBox(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  Define x0.i
  Define y0.i
  Define x1.i
  Define y1.i
  Define dx0.i
  Define dy0.i
  Define dx1.i
  Define dy1.i
  Define k.i
  Define n.i
  Define drawn.i

  If w < 1 Or h < 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  x0 = x
  y0 = y
  x1 = x + w
  y1 = y + h

  If nlClipOn = 1
    If x0 < nlClipX
      x0 = nlClipX
    EndIf
    If y0 < nlClipY
      y0 = nlClipY
    EndIf
    If x1 > (nlClipX + nlClipW)
      x1 = nlClipX + nlClipW
    EndIf
    If y1 > (nlClipY + nlClipH)
      y1 = nlClipY + nlClipH
    EndIf
    If x1 <= x0 Or y1 <= y0
      nlDropped = nlDropped + 1
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    If x0 <> x Or y0 <> y
      nlNarrowed = nlNarrowed + 1
    ElseIf x1 <> (x + w) Or y1 <> (y + h)
      nlNarrowed = nlNarrowed + 1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  If nlDmgOn = 0
    nlFills = nlFills + 1
    nlPixels = nlPixels + ((x1 - x0) * (y1 - y0))
    DisplayFillRect(x0, y0, x1 - x0, y1 - y0, colour)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; THE DAMAGE-LIMITED PATH.
  ;
  ; The early-out first, because it is what the overwhelming majority
  ; of a screen's primitives take: on a frame where one button changed,
  ; every one of the other four hundred boxes ends here having cost one
  ; DamageHits call. That call is the entire price of a primitive that
  ; does not need to be drawn, and it is the number damage.pi4's
  ; 27-microsecond-per-rectangle derivation is built out of.
  If DamageHits(x0, y0, x1 - x0, y1 - y0) = 0
    nlDmgSkipped = nlDmgSkipped + 1
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; Drawn once per damage rectangle it touches, clipped to each. This
  ; is only correct because damage.pi4 keeps its rectangles DISJOINT -
  ; overlapping ones would paint the shared pixels twice, which is
  ; merely wasteful for today's opaque fill and wrong for the alpha
  ; blend stage 1 adds. That invariant is asserted by
  ; pi4DamageSelfTest.pi4 rather than assumed here.
  ; EACH ACCESSOR IS CALLED ONCE PER RECTANGLE AND THE RESULT IS KEPT.
  ; A first version wrote "If DamageRectX(k) > dx0 : dx0 = DamageRectX(k)"
  ; four times over, which is sixteen procedure calls per primitive per
  ; rectangle. On a backend where a call plus its statically-allocated
  ; locals is thirty-odd instructions, a four-rectangle frame over a
  ; thousand primitives was paying about sixty-four thousand calls -
  ; roughly a millisecond and a half of pure accounting, on the very
  ; measurement (9a, the floor) that exists to find out what the
  ; accounting costs. Four calls, four locals.
  n = DamageCount()
  k = 0
  drawn = 0
  While k < n
    dx0 = DamageRectX(k)
    dy0 = DamageRectY(k)
    dx1 = dx0 + DamageRectW(k)
    dy1 = dy0 + DamageRectH(k)
    If x0 > dx0
      dx0 = x0
    EndIf
    If y0 > dy0
      dy0 = y0
    EndIf
    If x1 < dx1
      dx1 = x1
    EndIf
    If y1 < dy1
      dy1 = y1
    EndIf
    If dx1 > dx0
      If dy1 > dy0
        nlFills = nlFills + 1
        nlPixels = nlPixels + ((dx1 - dx0) * (dy1 - dy0))
        DisplayFillRect(dx0, dy0, dx1 - dx0, dy1 - dy0, colour)
        drawn = drawn + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf
    k = k + 1
  Wend
  If drawn > 1
    nlDmgSplit = nlDmgSplit + 1
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; Neon_Text, GlUIChrome.pbi:306-308.
;
; TWO PATHS, AND THE SPLIT IS DELIBERATE. Unclipped text is one
; DisplayTextClear call, because that is the primitive the plan names
; for this stage and it should appear in the program literally. Clipped
; text walks the string itself and calls DisplayCharClear per glyph,
; which is exactly DisplayTextClear's own loop (display.pi4:3069-3088)
; with one rectangle test added - because a whole-string call cannot
; drop the glyphs that fall outside a caller's clip rectangle, and
; display.pi4 has no caller clip rectangle to give it.
Procedure NlText(x.i, y.i, *s, colour.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  Define cx.i
  Define cw.i
  Define ch.i
  Define adv.i

  ; The one-call path survives only when NEITHER the widget scissor nor
  ; the damage list has anything to say. Both of them work a cell at a
  ; time, and DisplayTextClear cannot be asked to skip cells.
  If nlClipOn = 0
    If nlDmgOn = 0
      nlTexts = nlTexts + 1
      nlPixels = nlPixels + (DisplayTextWidth(*s) * DisplayCharHeight())
      DisplayTextClear(x, y, *s, colour)
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  EndIf

  cw = DisplayCharWidth()
  ch = DisplayCharHeight()

  ; ONE TEST FOR THE WHOLE RUN BEFORE WALKING IT. A status bar caption
  ; is ninety glyphs; asking the damage list ninety separate times when
  ; the answer for the run as a whole is "no" turns a rejected string
  ; into ninety rejections. DisplayTextWidth walks the string to
  ; measure it, which is cheap next to that and is the same measurement
  ; NlTextRight and NlTextCentre have already made by the time they get
  ; here anyway.
  If nlDmgOn = 1
    If DamageHits(x, y, DisplayTextWidth(*s), ch) = 0
      nlDmgSkipped = nlDmgSkipped + 1
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
  EndIf

  i = 0
  cx = x
  Repeat
    c = PeekB(*s + i) & $FF
    If c = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    ; The WHOLE cell has to be inside. See the header: this is
    ; glyph-granular and stage 1's clip is not. Written as four separate
    ; tests rather than one compound condition because the house rule
    ; wants full comparisons on both sides of every Or, and four short
    ; branches read better than the parenthesised chain that would be.
    adv = 0
    If nlClipOn = 1
      If cx < nlClipX
        adv = cw
      ElseIf (cx + cw) > (nlClipX + nlClipW)
        adv = cw
      ElseIf y < nlClipY
        adv = cw
      ElseIf (y + ch) > (nlClipY + nlClipH)
        adv = cw
      EndIf
      If adv <> 0
        nlGlyphsCut = nlGlyphsCut + 1
      EndIf
    EndIf

    ; THE DAMAGE TEST IS PER CELL AND THE CELL IS DRAWN WHOLE.
    ;
    ; A glyph that straddles the edge of a damage rectangle is drawn in
    ; full, so a few of its pixels land outside the damage. That is
    ; harmless and it is worth saying why rather than leaving it to be
    ; rediscovered: DisplayCharClear is transparent - it paints the lit
    ; pixels and leaves the rest - and the background outside the
    ; damage was, by definition, already correct. Painting the same
    ; glyph over the same unchanged background produces the same
    ; pixels.
    ;
    ; WHAT IT DOES MEAN IS THAT THE CALLER MUST REPORT THE WHOLE TEXT
    ; RUN AS DAMAGE, before and after. A string that got shorter leaves
    ; its tail on the screen if only the new extent was reported,
    ; because nothing erases outside a damage rectangle. That is the
    ; classic dirty-rectangle ghost and it reads as a font bug.
    If adv = 0
      If nlDmgOn = 1
        If DamageHits(cx, y, cw, ch) = 0
          adv = cw
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf

    If adv = 0
      nlGlyphs = nlGlyphs + 1
      nlPixels = nlPixels + (cw * ch)
      adv = DisplayCharClear(cx, y, c, colour)
      If adv < 1
        adv = cw
      EndIf
    EndIf
    cx = cx + adv
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure.i NlTextWidth(*s)
  ProcedureReturn DisplayTextWidth(*s)
EndProcedure

; Neon_TextRight, GlUIChrome.pbi:310-312.
Procedure NlTextRight(xRight.i, y.i, *s, colour.i)
  NlText(xRight - NlTextWidth(*s), y, *s, colour)
EndProcedure

; Neon_TextCentre, GlUIChrome.pbi:314-316.
Procedure NlTextCentre(x.i, w.i, y.i, *s, colour.i)
  NlText(x + ((w - NlTextWidth(*s)) / 2), y, *s, colour)
EndProcedure

; Neon_Frame, GlUIChrome.pbi:330-335. Four boxes, corners written twice,
; exactly as the original.
Procedure NlFrame(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  NlBox(x, y, w, 1, colour)
  NlBox(x, y + h - 1, w, 1, colour)
  NlBox(x, y, 1, h, colour)
  NlBox(x + w - 1, y, 1, h, colour)
EndProcedure

; Neon_Glow, GlUIChrome.pbi:323-328. Four nested boxes.
;
; THIS PROCEDURE IS LIE 1, AND IT IS WORTH LOOKING AT WHAT SURVIVED.
; The original's four boxes carry alpha strength/1.5, /3, /4.5 and /6 -
; between 2% and 7% at the default strength of 0.10. Here the four
; rectangles are drawn at full opacity in the order the original draws
; them, largest first, so the inner ones overwrite the outer ones and
; what is left on the screen is a hard four-pixel band of the glow
; colour. The GEOMETRY is faithful and only the alpha is gone, which is
; why the halo is exactly four pixels wide and not some other number.
Procedure NlGlow(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, colour.i)
  Define i.i
  For i = 4 To 1 Step -1
    NlBox(x - i, y - i, w + (i * 2), h + (i * 2), colour)
  Next
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE WIDGETS
;
;  Each of these is a transcription of the procedure named above it.
;  Interaction is gone - there is no mouse on this board yet (section 7
;  of the plan) - so the states a real frame would compute from the
;  pointer are passed in instead, which is what lets one static screen
;  show a hovered row, a selected row and a disabled row at once.
; ======================================================================

; Neon_Panel, GlUIChrome.pbi:339-348. Five boxes and a text.
Procedure NlPanel(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, *title, accent.i, headerH.i)
  NlBox(x, y, w, h, cPanel)
  NlBox(x, y, w, headerH, cHead)
  NlBox(x, y, 3, headerH, accent)
  NlBox(x, y + headerH, w, 1, cLine)
  ; The original upper-cases the title (UCase, :345). The titles below
  ; are written upper-case in the source instead, because there is no
  ; string library in this build and a case conversion is not what is
  ; being proved.
  NlText(x + 12, y + 5, *title, accent)
  NlFrame(x, y, w, h, cLine)
EndProcedure

; Neon_Button, GlUIChrome.pbi:350-374.
;
; hover is 0 or 1 rather than the 0..1 fade Neon_Animate produces
; (:354), because the fade is an alpha and there is no alpha. A half-way
; hover and a full hover are the same picture here. That is LIE 1 again,
; in its second costume.
Procedure NlButton(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, *label, lit.i, enabled.i, hover.i)
  Define face.i
  Define ink.i
  Define th.i

  face = cLine
  ink  = cText
  If lit = 1
    face = cBtnLit
    ink  = cBtnLitText
  EndIf
  If enabled = 0
    ink = cFaint
  EndIf

  ; :366 - the hovered button is glowed in the accent colour BEFORE the
  ; face is drawn, so the face covers the inner part of the glow and
  ; what is left is a ring. With alpha that ring is a lift. Without it,
  ; it is a halo.
  If hover = 1
    NlGlow(x, y, w, h, cAcc)
  EndIf

  NlBox(x, y, w, h, face)
  NlBox(x, y, w, 1, cBtnTop)                 ; :369
  NlBox(x, y + h - 1, w, 1, cBg)             ; :370
  th = DisplayCharHeight()
  NlTextCentre(x, w, y + ((h - th) / 2), *label, ink)
EndProcedure

; The menu bar's root tab, GlUIChrome.pbi:687-733, with the hit testing
; and the open/close state machine removed and the state passed in.
; Returns the tab's width so the caller can advance its cursor, which is
; what Neon_MenuCursorX does at :701.
Procedure.i NlMenuTab(x.i, y.i, h.i, *title, isOpen.i)
  Define tabW.i
  Define th.i

  ; :696-697 - tabW is the measured text width plus 24. Measured, not
  ; declared, exactly as the original: the whole point of LIE 2 is that
  ; the measurement is honest about a font that is the wrong one.
  tabW = NlTextWidth(*title) + 24
  th = DisplayCharHeight()

  If isOpen = 1
    NlBox(x, y, tabW, h, cAccTab)                        ; :725
    NlBox(x, y + h - 2, tabW, 2, cAcc)                   ; :726
    NlTextCentre(x, tabW, y + ((h - th) / 2), *title, cAcc)
  Else
    NlTextCentre(x, tabW, y + ((h - th) / 2), *title, cText)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn tabW
EndProcedure

; A drop-down row, GlUIChrome.pbi:817-897. rowH is 24 in the original
; (#NEON_MENU_ROWH, :610) and stays 24 here because Neon_UIScale is 1.
Procedure NlMenuItem(x.i, y.i, w.i, *label, *shortcut, enabled.i, checked.i, hovered.i)
  Define ink.i
  Define th.i
  Define ty.i
  Define mu.i
  Define mx.i
  Define my.i

  If hovered = 1
    NlBox(x + 1, y, w - 2, 24, cAccRow)                  ; :856
  EndIf

  ink = cText
  If enabled = 0
    ink = cFaint
  EndIf
  th = DisplayCharHeight()
  ty = y + ((24 - th) / 2)

  If checked = 1
    ; :869-885 - SEVEN COLUMNS, NOT THREE RECTANGLES, and the original's
    ; comment explains at length why: three rectangles read as a return
    ; arrow. markU is Neon_S(1.5) with a floor of 1.5; at scale 1 that
    ; is 1.5, and there are no fractional pixels here, so it is 2. The
    ; check is therefore 14 pixels wide rather than 10.5 - a rounding
    ; consequence of the .f -> .i conversion, visible, and the kind of
    ; thing stage 3 has to decide deliberately rather than inherit.
    mu = 2
    mx = x + 4
    my = y + ((24 - (mu * 7)) / 2)
    NlBox(mx,            my + (mu * 2), mu, mu * 3, cGrn)
    NlBox(mx + mu,       my + (mu * 3), mu, mu * 3, cGrn)
    NlBox(mx + (mu * 2), my + (mu * 4), mu, mu * 3, cGrn)
    NlBox(mx + (mu * 3), my + (mu * 3), mu, mu * 3, cGrn)
    NlBox(mx + (mu * 4), my + (mu * 2), mu, mu * 3, cGrn)
    NlBox(mx + (mu * 5), my + mu,       mu, mu * 3, cGrn)
    NlBox(mx + (mu * 6), my,            mu, mu * 3, cGrn)
  EndIf

  NlText(x + 14 + 4, ty, *label, ink)                    ; :887
  If PeekB(*shortcut) & $FF <> 0
    NlTextRight(x + w - 14, ty + 1, *shortcut, cFaint)   ; :889
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; Neon_MenuSeparator, GlUIChrome.pbi:899-913. One box, inset ten pixels
; each side, on the centre line of a nine-pixel row.
Procedure NlMenuSeparator(x.i, y.i, w.i)
  NlBox(x + 10, y + (9 / 2), w - 20, 1, cLine)
EndProcedure

; One tab of Neon_TabStrip, GlUIChrome.pbi:1111-1154. Returns its width.
Procedure.i NlStripTab(x.i, y.i, h.i, *label, isActive.i, hovered.i)
  Define tabW.i
  Define th.i

  tabW = NlTextWidth(*label) + 26                        ; :1115
  th = DisplayCharHeight()

  If isActive = 1
    NlBox(x, y, tabW, h - 1, cPanel)                     ; :1127
    NlBox(x, y, 1, h - 1, cLine)                         ; :1128
    NlBox(x + tabW - 1, y, 1, h - 1, cLine)              ; :1129
    NlText(x + 12, y + ((h - th) / 2), *label, cText)
    NlBox(x, y, tabW, 2, cAcc)                           ; :1158, the indicator
  Else
    If hovered = 1
      NlBox(x, y + 2, tabW, h - 3, cHead)                ; :1133
    EndIf
    NlText(x + 12, y + ((h - th) / 2), *label, cDim)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn tabW
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE LIST, GlUIChrome.pbi:1280-1363.
;
; THE GEOMETRY LIVES IN GLOBALS AND THAT IS NOT A SHORTCUT - it is what
; the original does. Neon_ListBegin stores x, y, w, h and RowH into
; Neon_ListX..Neon_ListRowH (:1287-1292) and Neon_ListItem reads them
; back; the item call takes only the things that change per row. This
; transcription had to arrive at the same shape for a second reason as
; well, and it is worth recording because it will constrain the real
; port too: THIS BACKEND PASSES AT MOST EIGHT ARGUMENTS. Nine and up
; would have to travel on the stack and that path is not implemented -
; the compiler refuses with a named error rather than emitting a wrong
; call, which is the project's philosophy showing up in the toolchain.
; A first draft of this procedure took ten and had to be split.
;
; state folds three booleans into one argument for the same reason:
;   0 a plain row      1 selected      2 a group heading      3 hovered
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global nlListX.i
Global nlListY.i
Global nlListW.i
Global nlListH.i
Global nlListRowH.i

Procedure NlListBegin(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, rowH.i)
  nlListX = x
  nlListY = y
  nlListW = w
  nlListH = h
  nlListRowH = rowH
  NlBox(x, y, w, h, cListBg)                 ; :1307, the well
  ; :1308 - the list clips to its own well, MINUS the scrollbar gutter,
  ; so the bar itself is drawn outside the clip by NlListEnd.
  NlClipSet(x, y, w - 10, h)
EndProcedure

; Neon_ListItem, GlUIChrome.pbi:1312-1353. Called inside the list's clip
; rectangle; rows above and below the well are cut by NlBox and dropped
; by NlText rather than by a visibility test, which is the point of
; drawing it this way.
Procedure NlListItem(y.i, *label, *detail, depth.i, state.i)
  Define ink.i
  Define th.i
  Define barW.i

  barW = nlListW - 10                        ; :1332, the scrollbar's gutter

  If state = 1
    NlBox(nlListX, y, barW, nlListRowH, cAccTab)   ; :1332
    NlBox(nlListX, y, 2, nlListRowH, cAcc)         ; :1333
  ElseIf state = 3
    ; :1335-1336 draws this at alpha = hover. Opaque here. LIE 1.
    NlBox(nlListX, y, barW, nlListRowH, cHead)
  EndIf

  ink = cText
  If state = 2
    ink = cAmb                               ; :1342
  ElseIf state = 1
    ink = cAcc                               ; :1344
  EndIf

  th = DisplayCharHeight()
  NlText(nlListX + 8 + (depth * 14), y + ((nlListRowH - th) / 2), *label, ink)  ; :1347
  If PeekB(*detail) & $FF <> 0
    NlTextRight(nlListX + nlListW - 16, y + ((nlListRowH - th) / 2), *detail, cFaint)  ; :1349
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; Neon_ScrollBarV, GlUIChrome.pbi:435-488, with the dragging removed.
; The thumb's height and position are the original's arithmetic in
; integers: thumbH = h * visible / total, floored at 18; thumbY = y +
; travel * top / maxTop. Written as one multiply then one divide, so the
; integer division happens once and at the end - the same discipline
; Q16.16 exists to make unnecessary in stage 3.
Procedure NlScrollBarV(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, total.i, visible.i, top.i, hot.i)
  Define thumbH.i
  Define travel.i
  Define thumbY.i
  Define maxTop.i
  Define colour.i

  NlBox(x, y, w, h, cBg)                     ; :437, the track

  If total <= visible Or total < 1
    ProcedureReturn                          ; :438-446, an inert track
  EndIf

  maxTop = total - visible
  thumbH = (h * visible) / total
  If thumbH < 18
    thumbH = 18                              ; :453
  EndIf
  travel = h - thumbH
  thumbY = y + ((travel * top) / maxTop)

  colour = cThumb
  If hot = 1
    colour = cAccThumb                       ; :485
  EndIf
  NlBox(x + 2, thumbY, w - 4, thumbH, colour)
EndProcedure

; Neon_ListEnd, GlUIChrome.pbi:1355-1363. Drops the clip, then draws the
; scrollbar in the ten-pixel gutter the clip deliberately left for it.
; total and top are passed in because there is no per-frame row count to
; remember here - the screen is drawn once.
Procedure NlListEnd(total.i, top.i)
  NlClipClear()                              ; :1357
  NlScrollBarV(nlListX + nlListW - 10, nlListY, 10, nlListH, total, nlListH / nlListRowH, top, 1)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE MUTABLE STATE - what makes this a UI rather than a picture
;
;  STAGE 0 DREW ONE FRAME AND THE STATES WERE WRITTEN INTO THE DRAWING
;  CODE: button three was the hovered one because the call said so.
;  Stage 0.6 needs to draw the SAME screen with one thing different, so
;  the three things that change now live here and NlDrawScreen reads
;  them. That is the only change to the stage 0 picture's meaning: with
;  these at their initial values the screen is pixel-identical to what
;  stage 0 drew, plus the text field, and the counters below prove it.
; ======================================================================

Global nlHoverBtn.i = 3     ; which button wears the glow. -1 for none.
Global nlListTop.i = 6      ; the list's first visible row
Global nlCaretOn.i = 1      ; the text field's caret

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHERE THE DAMAGEABLE WIDGETS ENDED UP.
;
; Filled in by NlDrawScreen as it lays the screen out, and read by the
; frame driver to build damage rectangles. NOT computed twice.
;
; The layout is resolution-dependent - this board reports 1824 x 984
; after overscan, not the 1920 x 1080 that was asked for - so every one
; of these is a number the drawing code worked out at run time. A frame
; driver carrying its own copy of the arithmetic would agree on this
; board and disagree on the next one, and the symptom would be a widget
; that repaints correctly at one resolution and ghosts at another.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global nlBtnX.i             ; the first button's left edge
Global nlBtnY.i
Global nlBtnW.i
Global nlBtnH.i
Global nlBtnStep.i          ; x advance from one button to the next
Global nlCaretX.i           ; the text field's caret, set by NlTextField
Global nlCaretY.i
Global nlCaretW.i
Global nlCaretH.i
Global nlWellX.i            ; the list's well, the scroll case
Global nlWellY.i
Global nlWellW.i
Global nlWellH.i

; NlIsHover exists because "hover = nlHoverBtn = 0" would be an
; assignment inside an argument list in this dialect and there is no
; boolean expression to hand instead. A named predicate is clearer than
; four locals computed above the call, and it keeps NlButton's argument
; count at eight - which is this backend's hard ceiling, as the note on
; NlListItem records.
Procedure.i NlIsHover(n.i)
  If nlHoverBtn = n
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; A TEXT FIELD WITH A CARET - the smallest thing a UI changes.
;
; THIS ONE WIDGET IS NOT A LINE-BY-LINE TRANSCRIPTION AND IT WOULD BE
; DISHONEST TO CITE IT AS ONE. Every other widget above carries a
; GlUIChrome.pbi line number because the port plan quotes those lines.
; GlUIChrome.pbi ITSELF IS NOT IN THIS REPOSITORY - it is the host-side
; NeonArcade engine - so what is known about Neon_TextInput here is
; what the plan records: that it is at GlUIChrome.pbi:1250, that it
; scissors (plan section 0.2), that it is one of only two places in the
; whole chrome kit that reads the keyboard (plan section 7.1), and that
; its caret blinks on Sin(Neon_CaretPhase) at :1256.
;
; So the shape below is composed from the chrome kit's own vocabulary -
; a well, a frame, a text run and a two-pixel bar - and it is not
; claimed to be pixel-identical to the original. It is here because
; STAGE 0.6 needs the SMALLEST possible change to a real screen, and a
; blinking caret is that: two pixels wide by one character cell tall,
; against 1.79 million pixels of surface.
;
; caretOn is a bool and not a phase, for the same reason NlButton's
; hover is: the original fades it with a sine and there is no alpha to
; fade with. LIE 1, in its third costume.
;
; IT RECORDS ITS OWN CARET GEOMETRY INTO GLOBALS, and that is the whole
; discipline of the damage layer in one place. The frame driver below
; must damage exactly the rectangle this procedure is going to paint,
; and the only way to be sure of that is for the same code to decide
; both. A frame driver that recomputed "the caret is at x + 8 + the
; text width" would be a second copy of a layout rule, and the first
; time somebody changed the 8 the caret would leave a ghost.
Procedure NlTextField(x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i, *text, focused.i, caretOn.i)
  Define th.i
  Define ty.i
  Define frame.i

  th = DisplayCharHeight()
  ty = y + ((h - th) / 2)

  NlBox(x, y, w, h, cListBg)
  frame = cLine
  If focused = 1
    frame = cAcc
  EndIf
  NlFrame(x, y, w, h, frame)
  NlText(x + 8, ty, *text, cText)

  nlCaretX = x + 8 + NlTextWidth(*text)
  nlCaretY = ty
  nlCaretW = 2
  nlCaretH = th

  If caretOn = 1
    NlBox(nlCaretX, nlCaretY, nlCaretW, nlCaretH, cAcc)
  EndIf
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE SCREEN
;
;  One frame of a plausible application, laid out against whatever
;  geometry the firmware actually gave us - NOT against 1920x1080. This
;  board reports 1824x984 after overscan, and a diagnostic that assumed
;  the number it asked for would draw half a picture and blame the
;  library.
; ======================================================================

Procedure NlDrawScreen()
  Define w.i
  Define h.i
  Define menuY.i
  Define menuH.i
  Define stripY.i
  Define stripH.i
  Define statusH.i
  Define statusY.i
  Define contentY.i
  Define contentH.i
  Define gut.i
  Define lx.i
  Define lw.i
  Define rx.i
  Define rw.i
  Define cx.i
  Define dx.i
  Define dy.i
  Define dw.i
  Define dh.i
  Define ry.i
  Define listX.i
  Define listY.i
  Define listW.i
  Define listH.i
  Define rowH.i
  Define rowTop.i
  Define dsY.i
  Define dsH.i
  Define bx.i
  Define by.i
  Define bw.i
  Define bh.i
  Define th.i

  w = DisplayWidth()
  h = DisplayHeight()
  th = DisplayCharHeight()

  menuY   = 0
  menuH   = 30
  stripY  = menuH
  stripH  = 28
  statusH = 24
  statusY = h - statusH
  gut     = 10
  contentY = stripY + stripH + gut
  contentH = statusY - gut - contentY

  ; ---- the ground ---------------------------------------------------
  NlBox(0, 0, w, h, cBg)

  ; ---- the menu bar, GlUIChrome.pbi:650-683 --------------------------
  NlBox(0, menuY, w, menuH, cHead)                       ; :676
  NlBox(0, menuY + menuH - 1, w, 1, cLine)               ; :677

  cx = 8                                                 ; :653, x + Neon_S(8)
  cx = cx + NlMenuTab(cx, menuY, menuH, "File",  0)
  cx = cx + NlMenuTab(cx, menuY, menuH, "Edit",  0)
  dx = cx
  cx = cx + NlMenuTab(cx, menuY, menuH, "Board", 1)      ; this one is open
  cx = cx + NlMenuTab(cx, menuY, menuH, "Tools", 0)
  cx = cx + NlMenuTab(cx, menuY, menuH, "Help",  0)

  ; ---- the tab strip, GlUIChrome.pbi:1095-1162 -----------------------
  NlBox(0, stripY, w, stripH, cBg)                       ; :1097
  NlBox(0, stripY + stripH - 1, w, 1, cLine)             ; :1098
  cx = 4                                                 ; :1105
  cx = cx + NlStripTab(cx, stripY, stripH, "main.pmf",   0, 0)
  cx = cx + NlStripTab(cx, stripY, stripH, "display.pi4", 1, 0)
  cx = cx + NlStripTab(cx, stripY, stripH, "anvil.pi4",  0, 1)

  ; ---- the left panel and its list -----------------------------------
  lx = gut
  lw = 380
  NlPanel(lx, contentY, lw, contentH, "PROJECT", cAcc, 26)

  listX = lx + 8
  listY = contentY + 26 + 8
  listW = lw - 16
  rowH  = 22
  rowTop = nlListTop                        ; the list's scroll position, now state

  ; THE WELL IS DELIBERATELY TOO SMALL, and this was a real defect caught
  ; by replaying the layout arithmetic before the board ever saw it. The
  ; first version gave the list the whole panel: at 1824x984 that is 840
  ; pixels, thirty-eight rows of space for twenty-eight rows of content.
  ; Nothing overflowed, so the clip was only ever handed rectangles that
  ; were already inside it, the scrollbar took its inert-track path
  ; (GlUIChrome.pbi:438-446) and drew no thumb, and the screen would have
  ; quietly proved nothing while looking correct. A test that cannot fail
  ; is not a test.
  ;
  ; Sixteen rows, and twenty-eight to put in them. The panel's remaining
  ; height goes to a detail strip underneath, which is what a real file
  ; browser does with it anyway.
  listH = (16 * rowH) + 8
  If listH > (contentH - 26 - 16)
    listH = contentH - 26 - 16               ; a short panel still fits
  EndIf

  ; The well's geometry, recorded for the scroll frame's damage. A
  ; scroll moves every row in the well, so the damage IS the well - see
  ; the note on the scroll case in section 9.
  nlWellX = listX
  nlWellY = listY
  nlWellW = listW
  nlWellH = listH

  ; Neon_ListBegin, GlUIChrome.pbi:1286-1309 - the well, then the clip.
  NlListBegin(listX, listY, listW, listH, rowH)

  ; TWENTY-EIGHT ROWS INTO A WELL THAT HOLDS FEWER, starting six rows
  ; above the top of the well. So the first visible row and the last
  ; one are both PARTLY outside it, which is the whole reason the clip
  ; is here. The rows above and below are drawn and thrown away rather
  ; than skipped by a visibility test, deliberately: a clip that is only
  ; ever handed rectangles that were already inside it has proved
  ; nothing. The counters at the end of the run say how many boxes it
  ; narrowed and how many glyph cells it dropped.
  ; ROW 5 AND ROW 22 ARE THE TWO PARTIAL ROWS, at the top and bottom
  ; edges of the well, and they are the ones given a hover on purpose: a
  ; hovered row is the only list state that paints a background box, so
  ; those two are what make the BOX clip visible rather than merely
  ; correct. You should see a sliver of a bar with no text in it at each
  ; edge - the box cut to the pixel, the glyph cell dropped whole.
  ; Row 12 (mmu.pi4) is the selection, and it sits well inside the view
  ; because a selection scrolled out of sight proves nothing. An earlier
  ; draft had it on row 4, six rows above the top of the well.
  ry = listY - (rowTop * rowH)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  0), "RaspberryPi4",      "", 0, 2)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  1), "Lib",               "31", 1, 2)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  2), "bits.pi4",          "23 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  3), "clocks.pi4",        "9 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  4), "display.pi4",       "166 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  5), "emmc.pi4",          "92 KB", 2, 3)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  6), "fat.pi4",           "181 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  7), "genet.pi4",         "133 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  8), "gpio.pi4",          "29 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH *  9), "mailbox.pi4",       "83 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 10), "math.pi4",          "70 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 11), "memory.pi4",        "67 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 12), "mmu.pi4",           "69 KB", 2, 1)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 13), "timer.pi4",         "18 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 14), "uart.pi4",          "54 KB", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 15), "Examples",          "", 1, 2)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 16), "Diagnostics",       "30", 2, 2)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 17), "pi4DisplayProbe",   "", 3, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 18), "pi4MmuState",       "", 3, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 19), "pi4NeonLiar",       "here", 3, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 20), "pi4TimerProbe",     "", 3, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 21), "Monitor",           "", 1, 2)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 22), "anvil.pi4",         "", 2, 3)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 23), "Intrinsics",        "", 1, 2)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 24), "bcm2711.def",       "", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 25), "Reference",         "", 1, 2)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 26), "README.md",         "", 2, 0)
  NlListItem(ry + (rowH * 27), "Boards",            "", 0, 2)

  ; Neon_ListEnd, GlUIChrome.pbi:1355-1363 - clip off, then the bar in
  ; the gutter the clip left for it. total 28 against visible 16, so this
  ; one takes the real path and draws a thumb sixteen twenty-eighths of
  ; the track high, six rows down.
  NlListEnd(28, rowTop)

  ; ---- the detail strip, in the panel space the list did not take -----
  ; Neon_Frame plus text: no new primitive, and it gives the panel
  ; something to do below the list rather than a rectangle of nothing.
  dsY = listY + listH + 10
  dsH = (contentY + contentH) - dsY - 8
  If dsH > 40
    NlFrame(listX, dsY, listW, dsH, cLine)
    NlText(listX + 10, dsY + 8,      "display.pi4", cAcc)
    NlText(listX + 10, dsY + 8 + th, "166 KB   proven on silicon", cDim)
    If dsH > (8 + (th * 3))
      NlText(listX + 10, dsY + 8 + (th * 2), "do not modify", cAmb)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ; ---- the right panel -----------------------------------------------
  rx = lx + lw + gut
  rw = w - rx - gut
  NlPanel(rx, contentY, rw, contentH, "SERIAL MONITOR", cAmb, 26)

  ry = contentY + 26 + 12
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 0), "PureMetal Forge - Anvil", cDim)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 1), "screen up - real geometry is on the serial line", cDim)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 2), "mailbox ok - ARM clock queried, not assumed", cDim)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 3), "SCTLR_EL2 $0000000030C50830 - MMU off, caches off", cGrn)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 4), "stage 0: two primitives and a scissor", cText)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 5), "stage 0.5: four passes, see the serial line", cText)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 6), "no floating point was executed to draw this", cAmb)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 7), "warning: every glow below is opaque", cRed)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 8), "note: the accent colour is 90,189,208", cAcc)
  NlText(rx + 14, ry + (th * 9), "note: the violet entry is 169,152,224", cVio)

  ; ---- the buttons, one of each state --------------------------------
  ; MEASURED FROM THE PANEL, not hardcoded at 150. The first version was
  ; hardcoded and the four buttons ran 420 pixels past the right edge of
  ; the panel on a 640-wide surface - which this board is not, but a
  ; layout that is only correct at one resolution is a layout that has
  ; not been thought about. Four buttons and three twelve-pixel gaps.
  bw = ((rw - 28) - (12 * 3)) / 4
  If bw > 150
    bw = 150
  EndIf
  bh = 30
  by = contentY + contentH - bh - 16
  bx = rx + 14

  ; Recorded for the hover frame's damage, BEFORE the buttons are
  ; drawn, so that the frame driver and the drawing code cannot
  ; disagree about where they are.
  nlBtnX = bx
  nlBtnY = by
  nlBtnW = bw
  nlBtnH = bh
  nlBtnStep = bw + 12

  ; ---- the text field, above the buttons -------------------------------
  ; It is here because stage 0.6 needs the smallest change a real UI
  ; makes, and a blinking caret is it. See NlTextField's header for why
  ; this one widget carries no GlUIChrome.pbi line number.
  NlTextField(bx, by - 44, (bw * 3) + 24, 28, "COM7 115200", 1, nlCaretOn)

  NlButton(bx,                 by, bw, bh, "BUILD",   0, 1, NlIsHover(0))
  NlButton(bx + (bw + 12),     by, bw, bh, "UPLOAD",  1, 1, NlIsHover(1))
  NlButton(bx + (bw + 12) * 2, by, bw, bh, "MONITOR", 0, 0, NlIsHover(2))
  NlButton(bx + (bw + 12) * 3, by, bw, bh, "HOVERED", 0, 1, NlIsHover(3))

  ; ---- the status bar -------------------------------------------------
  NlBox(0, statusY, w, statusH, cHead)
  NlBox(0, statusY, w, 1, cLine)
  NlText(12, statusY + ((statusH - th) / 2), "pi4NeonLiar - stage 0", cDim)
  NlTextCentre(0, w, statusY + ((statusH - th) / 2), "LIE 1: every glow and hover is OPAQUE.   LIE 2: every glyph is 8px FIXED WIDTH.   Both intended.", cAmb)
  NlTextRight(w - 12, statusY + ((statusH - th) / 2), "display.pi4 unmodified", cDim)

  ; ---- the open drop-down, LAST, because it draws on top --------------
  ; GlUIChrome.pbi:772-774. dx is the x of the Board tab, remembered
  ; above; the drop-down hangs from the bottom of the bar (:750).
  dy = menuY + menuH
  dw = 300
  dh = (24 * 6) + 9 + 8
  NlGlow(dx, dy, dw, dh, cBlack)                         ; :772 - the shadow
  NlBox(dx, dy, dw, dh, cHead)                           ; :773
  NlFrame(dx, dy, dw, dh, cAccFrame)                     ; :774

  ry = dy + 4
  NlMenuItem(dx, ry,          dw, "Raspberry Pi 4",  "",       1, 1, 0)
  NlMenuItem(dx, ry + 24,     dw, "Raspberry Pi 5",  "",       0, 0, 0)
  NlMenuItem(dx, ry + 48,     dw, "RP2350",          "",       1, 0, 1)
  NlMenuSeparator(dx, ry + 72, dw)
  NlMenuItem(dx, ry + 81,     dw, "Rescan ports",    "Ctrl+R", 1, 0, 0)
  NlMenuItem(dx, ry + 105,    dw, "Board manager",   "Ctrl+B", 1, 0, 0)
  NlMenuItem(dx, ry + 129,    dw, "Properties",      "Alt+P",  1, 0, 0)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  STAGE 0.6 - THE FRAME DRIVER
;
;  Everything above draws a screen. This draws the SAME screen with one
;  widget different, repaints only what that changed, and times it
;  against the full redraw. The ratio between those two numbers is what
;  stage 0.6 is for.
; ======================================================================

Procedure NlCountersReset()
  nlFills = 0
  nlGlyphs = 0
  nlTexts = 0
  nlNarrowed = 0
  nlDropped = 0
  nlGlyphsCut = 0
  nlPixels = 0
  nlDmgSkipped = 0
  nlDmgSplit = 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; NlPaintFull - the baseline. Every primitive draws; the whole surface
; is flushed. Returns microseconds INCLUDING the flush, because a frame
; that has not been made visible is not a frame and comparing a draw
; against a draw-plus-flush would flatter the wrong one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NlPaintFull()
  Define t0.i
  NlCountersReset()
  nlDmgOn = 0
  t0 = Micros()
  NlDrawScreen()
  DisplayFlush()
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; NlPaintDamaged - the same traversal, damage-limited, and then a flush
; of ONLY the damaged rectangles.
;
; THE FLUSH IS PART OF THE SAVING AND IT IS EASY TO LEAVE OUT. With the
; MMU off, DisplayFlush() is 129,600 dc cvac instructions over a
; 1824 x 984 surface that are architecturally no-ops and still execute.
; With the MMU on and DRAM cacheable they are real work. Either way a
; damage-limited repaint that ended in a whole-surface flush would have
; given back a large part of what it saved, so the flush is per
; rectangle here and it is inside the timing.
;
; The widget tree is walked ONCE, whatever the rectangle count. See the
; note on nlDmgOn.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NlPaintDamaged()
  Define t0.i
  Define k.i
  Define n.i

  NlCountersReset()
  nlDmgOn = 1
  t0 = Micros()
  NlDrawScreen()
  n = DamageCount()
  k = 0
  While k < n
    DisplayFlushRect(DamageRectX(k), DamageRectY(k), DamageRectW(k), DamageRectH(k))
    k = k + 1
  Wend
  nlDmgOn = 0
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE THREE EVENTS, EACH DECLARING ITS OWN DAMAGE.
;
; Each of these is what a real event handler would do: change the state
; and say what that will repaint. They are separate from the drawing
; code and they read the geometry the drawing code recorded, which is
; the discipline the note on nlBtnX sets out.
;
; THE BUTTON DAMAGE IS THE BUTTON PLUS FOUR PIXELS ON EVERY SIDE, and
; the four is not a safety margin - it is Neon_Glow's ring
; (GlUIChrome.pbi:323-328), which is four nested boxes each one pixel
; further out. A damage rectangle of exactly the button would leave the
; halo of the button that LOST the hover still painted, as a hollow
; cyan frame around nothing. That is the ghost this whole layer has to
; be careful about, and it is four pixels wide.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure NlDamageButton(n.i)
  If n < 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  DamageAdd(nlBtnX + (nlBtnStep * n) - 4, nlBtnY - 4, nlBtnW + 8, nlBtnH + 8)
EndProcedure

Procedure NlEventHover(n.i)
  ; BOTH buttons are damaged, the one losing the hover and the one
  ; gaining it. A frame that damaged only the new one is the commonest
  ; dirty-rectangle bug there is.
  NlDamageButton(nlHoverBtn)
  nlHoverBtn = n
  NlDamageButton(n)
EndProcedure

Procedure NlEventCaret()
  ; The caret's own cell only. NlTextField recorded it on the last
  ; full paint; it does not move while the text does not change.
  nlCaretOn = 1 - nlCaretOn
  DamageAdd(nlCaretX, nlCaretY, nlCaretW, nlCaretH)
EndProcedure

Procedure NlEventScroll(rows.i)
  ; THE WHOLE WELL, AND THAT IS CORRECT RATHER THAN LAZY. A scroll of
  ; one row moves every pixel in the well; there is no smaller honest
  ; answer with a software fill. (There is a smaller answer with a
  ; hardware blitter - DisplayCopyRect the well up by one row and
  ; repaint the one row that is newly exposed - and that is exactly the
  ; kind of thing the DMA 2D engine in section 9.3 of the port plan is
  ; for. It is deliberately NOT done here, because this measurement is
  ; about the damage layer and not about a scrolling trick, and because
  ; mixing the two would make the scroll number unquotable.)
  ;
  ; The scrollbar thumb lives in the ten-pixel gutter OUTSIDE the clip
  ; (NlListBegin sets the clip to w - 10) and it moves too, so the
  ; damage is the well plus its gutter - which is nlWellW, the full
  ; width, because the well's own box already spans both.
  nlListTop = nlListTop + rows
  If nlListTop < 0
    nlListTop = 0
  EndIf
  If nlListTop > 12
    nlListTop = 12
  EndIf
  DamageAdd(nlWellX, nlWellY, nlWellW, nlWellH)
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE SHADOW-BUFFER QUESTION - MEASURED, NOT ARGUED
;
;  Section 6.3 of the port plan drops the engine's FBO and says: if the
;  measurement demands a shadow buffer, add it as a shadow buffer. This
;  is that measurement, and it is one question with one experiment.
;
;  THE QUESTION IS NOT "IS DOUBLE BUFFERING NICE". It is: DOES A STORE
;  INTO THE FRAMEBUFFER COST THE SAME AS A STORE INTO ORDINARY DRAM?
;
;  Everything else follows from the answer. If they are the same, a
;  shadow buffer can only add work - you write the pixel twice and copy
;  it once - and damage-repaint straight into the live framebuffer is
;  right. If the framebuffer is much slower, then the framebuffer is
;  the wall, composing into DRAM and copying out only the damaged
;  rectangles is the answer, and it is the DAMAGE LAYER that makes that
;  copy small enough to be worth doing.
;
;  IT IS WORTH SAYING WHY THIS IS NOT ALREADY KNOWN. Stage 0.5 measured
;  266 ms of opaque stores over 8.29 MB with DRAM mapped cacheable -
;  about 148 ns and 222 CPU cycles for one four-byte pixel, through a
;  loop whose own header claims 1.4 instructions per byte
;  (display.pi4:2386-2389). Those two cannot both be true. Either the
;  instruction count is wrong or those stores are not going to cacheable
;  memory at all, and nothing on this disk distinguishes them. THE SAME
;  LOOP AGAINST TWO DESTINATIONS DOES, in one pair of numbers.
;
;  THE SHADOW LIVES AT $01000000 and the address is chosen, not found.
;  Anvil's map starts the monitor at $00200000 and ends it wherever its
;  image ends, with the low payload window from the megabyte above that
;  up to $07EFFFFF - `map` at the prompt prints both. This image is
;  staged at that base and its stack grows DOWN from $03000000,
;  and mmu.pi4's tables are at $00600000. 16 MiB is inside the payload
;  window, above the image and its page tables, and 32 MiB below the
;  stack. A full 1824 x 984 x 4 surface is 7.18 MB, so the shadow ends
;  at about $016D8000 with room to spare in every direction.
;
;  IT IS NOT A Global Dim, AND THAT WAS THE FIRST ATTEMPT. Seven
;  megabytes of BSS on this backend is seven megabytes in the image
;  file, and the image reaches the board over a 115200-baud serial
;  line. That is about ten minutes of `receive` for a buffer whose
;  contents nobody cares about.
;
;  A PREDICTION, WRITTEN DOWN BEFORE THE RUN SO THAT IT CAN BE WRONG.
;  Stage 0.5's own numbers already point one way, and saying so in
;  advance is worth more than explaining the result afterwards:
;
;    read, loads only   1155.0 ms
;    clear, stores only  266.4 ms
;
;  Reads are 4.3x dearer than writes over the same 8.29 MB. That is
;  not what a slow INSTRUCTION LOOP looks like - a load loop and a
;  store loop cost about the same number of instructions, so a
;  codegen-bound pass would show the two roughly level. It is exactly
;  what DEVICE MEMORY looks like: stores post and retire, loads stall
;  to the far end and nothing gathers or combines.
;
;  SO THE PREDICTION IS THAT THE FRAMEBUFFER IS STILL UNCACHED EVEN
;  WITH THE MMU ON - that mmu.pi4's identity map does not give the
;  firmware's framebuffer region the Normal Cacheable attribute that
;  the rest of DRAM gets - and that section 10 will therefore report a
;  large ratio and a SHADOW-BUFFER verdict.
;
;  IF THE RATIO COMES BACK NEAR 1.0 THE PREDICTION IS WRONG and the
;  finding is bigger, not smaller: it would mean 222 cycles per pixel
;  is what this backend's store loop genuinely costs in cacheable
;  memory, and the whole problem moves to the code generator. Either
;  answer is worth the run. Neither is worth guessing.
; ======================================================================

#NL_SHADOW = $01000000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; NlPassStore - w pixels by rows rows of one colour at base/pitch.
;
; THE INNER LOOP IS DspRowFill32's, DELIBERATELY. Same eight-pixel
; unroll, same 64-bit PokeI, same masked doubling, same head pixel for
; alignment (display.pi4:2414-2455). The point of this measurement is
; to compare two DESTINATIONS, so everything else has to be identical -
; a naive one-pixel loop here would be measuring the loop.
;
; It is copied rather than called because DspRowFill32 takes a byte
; address computed from display.pi4's own globals and there is no way
; to point it at anything else. Copying a proven loop to move it to a
; different address is the smaller sin than adding a base parameter to
; a file that drives Anvil's console.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NlPassStore(base.i, pitch.i, w.i, rows.i, colour.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define y.i
  Define p.i
  Define n.i
  Define left.i
  Define wide.i
  Define c.i

  c = colour & $FFFFFFFF
  wide = c | (c << 32)

  t0 = Micros()
  y = 0
  While y < rows
    p = base + (y * pitch)
    left = w
    If (p & 7) <> 0
      PokeL(p, c)
      p = p + 4
      left = left - 1
    EndIf
    n = left >> 3
    While n > 0
      PokeI(p, wide)
      PokeI(p + 8, wide)
      PokeI(p + 16, wide)
      PokeI(p + 24, wide)
      p = p + 32
      n = n - 1
    Wend
    left = left & 7
    While left > 0
      PokeL(p, c)
      p = p + 4
      left = left - 1
    Wend
    y = y + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; NlPassCopy - the presentation step a shadow buffer needs: one
; rectangle out of the shadow and into the framebuffer, at the same
; coordinates.
;
; EIGHT ARGUMENTS IS THE CEILING ON THIS BACKEND, so the two pitches
; travel and the geometry is a rectangle rather than x, y, w, h plus
; both bases. That is why the source and destination rectangles are the
; same one: a shadow buffer that composed at a different offset from
; the panel would need a ninth argument, and there is no reason to
; want one.
;
; Wide 64-bit moves, two pixels at a time, for the same reason the fill
; is unrolled. No alignment head here: both bases are $100-aligned and
; both pitches are multiples of eight on this surface, so a rectangle
; starting at an even x is eight-aligned in both buffers. The odd-x
; case falls into the tail loop and is correct, just slower - and the
; damage rectangles this is asked to copy come from widget geometry,
; which is even far more often than not.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NlPassCopy(dst.i, dpitch.i, src.i, spitch.i, x.i, y.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define r.i
  Define d.i
  Define s.i
  Define n.i
  Define left.i

  t0 = Micros()
  r = 0
  While r < h
    d = dst + ((y + r) * dpitch) + (x * 4)
    s = src + ((y + r) * spitch) + (x * 4)
    left = w
    If (d & 7) <> 0
      PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
      d = d + 4
      s = s + 4
      left = left - 1
    EndIf
    n = left >> 2
    While n > 0
      PokeI(d, PeekI(s))
      PokeI(d + 8, PeekI(s + 8))
      d = d + 16
      s = s + 16
      n = n - 1
    Wend
    left = left & 3
    While left > 0
      PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
      d = d + 4
      s = s + 4
      left = left - 1
    Wend
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  THE PROOF THAT THE FAST FRAME IS THE RIGHT FRAME
;
;  A damage-limited repaint that is fast and wrong is worse than a slow
;  one, and it is wrong in a way nobody photographs: a stale pixel
;  outside the damage rectangle looks like a rendering artefact, three
;  layers away from the accounting mistake that caused it.
;
;  So the timing is not the deliverable on its own. This is the check
;  that goes with it, and it is the only one that can settle the
;  question completely:
;
;    1. draw state B in full, and keep a copy of the surface;
;    2. draw state A in full, over the top;
;    3. apply the event that turns A into B, declaring its damage, and
;       repaint ONLY that damage;
;    4. compare every pixel of the surface with the copy from step 1.
;
;  A single differing pixel means the event under-reported its damage.
;  Zero means the fast path and the slow path produce the same picture,
;  which is the whole claim.
;
;  IT COSTS A FULL-SURFACE COMPARE, which stage 0.5 measured as a read
;  pass at over a second on this board. That is why it runs once, on
;  one representative event, rather than after every frame in section 9
;  - and why the event it runs on is the HOVER rather than the caret.
;  The hover is the one with two damage rectangles, the one where a
;  four-pixel glow ring has to be accounted for on a widget that is
;  losing it, and therefore the one where an under-report is plausible.
;  A caret that failed this check would be a much stranger bug.
; ======================================================================

Global nlDiffX.i = -1
Global nlDiffY.i = -1

; A whole-surface copy, used to keep the reference picture. Wide moves,
; row by row against the pitch - never width * bpp, for the reason
; display.pi4:313-331 spends a section on.
Procedure.i NlSnapshot(dst.i, dpitch.i, src.i, spitch.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define r.i
  Define d.i
  Define s.i
  Define n.i

  t0 = Micros()
  r = 0
  While r < h
    d = dst + (r * dpitch)
    s = src + (r * spitch)
    n = w >> 1
    While n > 0
      PokeI(d, PeekI(s))
      d = d + 8
      s = s + 8
      n = n - 1
    Wend
    If (w & 1) = 1
      PokeL(d, PeekN(s))
    EndIf
    r = r + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

; Count the pixels that differ, and remember where the first one was.
;
; IT COUNTS RATHER THAN STOPPING AT THE FIRST. "One pixel differs" and
; "forty thousand differ" are different bugs - the first is an edge or
; an off-by-one in a damage rectangle, the second is a whole widget
; that was never repainted - and the count tells them apart without a
; second run. The coordinate is what says which widget.
Procedure.i NlDiff(a.i, apitch.i, b.i, bpitch.i, w.i, h.i)
  Define bad.i
  Define x.i
  Define y.i
  Define pa.i
  Define pb.i

  bad = 0
  nlDiffX = -1
  nlDiffY = -1
  y = 0
  While y < h
    pa = a + (y * apitch)
    pb = b + (y * bpitch)
    x = 0
    While x < w
      If (PeekN(pa) & $FFFFFFFF) <> (PeekN(pb) & $FFFFFFFF)
        If bad = 0
          nlDiffX = x
          nlDiffY = y
        EndIf
        bad = bad + 1
      EndIf
      pa = pa + 4
      pb = pb + 4
      x = x + 1
    Wend
    y = y + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn bad
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  STAGE 0.5 - THE FOUR PASSES
;
;  Each takes a row count so the same code can be probed over eight rows
;  and then run over the whole surface, and each returns the elapsed
;  microseconds from Micros() (timer.pi4:354).
;
;  THE ROW ADDRESS IS base + y * pitch, never base + y * width * bpp.
;  display.pi4's header spends a whole section on why (:313-331): the
;  wrong one does not blank the screen, it SHEARS the picture
;  diagonally, and it looks like a bad cable.
; ======================================================================

Procedure.i NlPassClear(rows.i, colour.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define w.i
  t0 = Micros()
  w = DisplayWidth()
  DisplayFillRect(0, 0, w, rows, colour)
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

Global nlReadAcc.i = 0

; Read every pixel and accumulate it. The accumulator is a global and is
; printed afterwards, so nothing about this loop can be argued away as
; dead - this backend does not dead-strip anyway (math.pi4:127-132
; records that including a library costs its whole size), but a
; measurement whose work might have been removed is not a measurement.
Procedure.i NlPassRead(rows.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define base.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define w.i
  Define y.i
  Define p.i
  Define n.i
  Define acc.i

  base  = DisplayBase()
  pitch = DisplayPitch()
  w     = DisplayWidth()
  acc   = 0

  t0 = Micros()
  y = 0
  While y < rows
    p = base + (y * pitch)
    n = w
    While n > 0
      acc = acc + (PeekN(p) & $FFFFFFFF)
      p = p + 4
      n = n - 1
    Wend
    y = y + 1
  Wend
  nlReadAcc = acc
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE BLEND, WRITTEN TO BE READ.
;
; dst = (src * a + dst * (255 - a)) >> 8, per channel, non-premultiplied
; - the one blend equation the whole engine needs, because
; glBlendFunc(GL_SRC_ALPHA, GL_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA) is set once at
; GlUtilities.pbi:718-719 and never changed again.
;
; >> 8 RATHER THAN / 255, AND THE OFF-BY-ONE IS REAL. With a = 255 this
; produces 254 where it should produce 255, so an "opaque" blend fades a
; white by one level. Stage 1 does not have this problem because
; GfxFill's opaque case takes the fast path and never reaches the blend
; at all (section 2.2 of the plan). It is written this way here because
; a divide by 255 per channel per pixel would be measuring a divider
; that no compositor would ship.
;
; The source colour is taken already PACKED, from DisplayRGB, and split
; the same way it was packed. That makes the pass correct whichever way
; DisplayColourSwap has the channels: red and blue could be the other
; way round throughout and this blend would still be blending each
; channel with itself.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NlPassBlendPlain(rows.i, src.i, sa.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define base.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define w.i
  Define y.i
  Define p.i
  Define n.i
  Define d.i
  Define ia.i
  Define ra.i
  Define ga.i
  Define ba.i
  Define dr.i
  Define dg.i
  Define db.i

  base  = DisplayBase()
  pitch = DisplayPitch()
  w     = DisplayWidth()
  ia = 255 - sa
  ra = ((src >> 16) & 255) * sa
  ga = ((src >> 8) & 255) * sa
  ba = (src & 255) * sa

  t0 = Micros()
  y = 0
  While y < rows
    p = base + (y * pitch)
    n = w
    While n > 0
      d  = PeekN(p) & $FFFFFFFF
      dr = (ra + (((d >> 16) & 255) * ia)) >> 8
      dg = (ga + (((d >> 8) & 255) * ia)) >> 8
      db = (ba + ((d & 255) * ia)) >> 8
      PokeL(p, (((($FF << 24) | (dr << 16)) | (dg << 8)) | db))
      p = p + 4
      n = n - 1
    Wend
    y = y + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE SAME BLEND, FUSED.
;
; Identical arithmetic, identical result, with the three per-channel
; locals gone. On a machine with a register allocator these two are the
; same program. On THIS backend they are not, and DspRowFill32's header
; says why in detail (display.pi4:2372-2384): procedure locals are
; statically allocated, so "dr = ..." is an adrp/add/str and every later
; mention of dr is an adrp/add/ldr. Three locals written once and read
; once is twelve address materialisations per pixel that the fused form
; does not pay.
;
; EVERY SHIFT IS PARENTHESISED. On this compiler & and | bind TIGHTER
; than << and >>, so "d >> 16 & 255" parses as "d >> (16 & 255)" and
; would silently be a different program. display.pi4's header calls this
; out as one of its three 64-bit traps (:416-421) and every shift in
; that file is parenthesised for the same reason, including the ones
; where it would not have mattered.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i NlPassBlendFused(rows.i, src.i, sa.i)
  Define t0.i
  Define base.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define w.i
  Define y.i
  Define p.i
  Define n.i
  Define d.i
  Define ia.i
  Define ra.i
  Define ga.i
  Define ba.i

  base  = DisplayBase()
  pitch = DisplayPitch()
  w     = DisplayWidth()
  ia = 255 - sa
  ra = ((src >> 16) & 255) * sa
  ga = ((src >> 8) & 255) * sa
  ba = (src & 255) * sa

  t0 = Micros()
  y = 0
  While y < rows
    p = base + (y * pitch)
    n = w
    While n > 0
      d = PeekN(p) & $FFFFFFFF
      PokeL(p, (((($FF << 24) | ((((ra + (((d >> 16) & 255) * ia)) >> 8)) << 16)) | ((((ga + (((d >> 8) & 255) * ia)) >> 8)) << 8)) | ((ba + ((d & 255) * ia)) >> 8)))
      p = p + 4
      n = n - 1
    Wend
    y = y + 1
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn Micros() - t0
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  REPORTING ONE PASS
;
;  Every derived figure is stated with the number it was derived from
;  beside it, so a wrong assumption is visible rather than folded in.
; ======================================================================

; bytes is the TRAFFIC, not the pixel count times four: a blend touches
; each pixel twice, once in and once out, and the whole point of the
; measurement is the memory traffic.
Procedure NlReport(us.i, pixels.i, bytes.i, hz.i)
  Define cycles.i

  PutLabel("elapsed            ")
  PutMs(us)
  UartWriteStr(" ms  (")
  PrintDec(us)
  UartWriteStr(" us)")
  PrintNl()

  If us < 1
    PutLabel("too fast to divide - the counter did not move")
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  PutLabel("traffic            ")
  PrintDec(bytes)
  UartWriteStr(" bytes")
  PrintNl()

  ; bytes per microsecond IS megabytes per second, with a megabyte
  ; meaning 1000000 bytes. Said, rather than left for the reader.
  PutLabel("rate               ")
  PutFix2((bytes * 100) / us)
  UartWriteStr(" MB/s   (decimal MB, i.e. bytes per microsecond)")
  PrintNl()

  PutLabel("ns per pixel       ")
  PutFix2((us * 100000) / pixels)
  PrintNl()

  If hz > 0
    cycles = (us * hz) / 1000000
    PutLabel("cycles per pixel   ")
    PutFix2((cycles * 100) / pixels)
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("cycles per byte    ")
    PutFix2((cycles * 100) / bytes)
    UartWriteStr("   (compare 1.4 instructions/byte, display.pi4:2386-2389)")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
EndProcedure


; ======================================================================
;  MAIN
; ======================================================================

; 1 = turn the MMU on with DRAM cacheable before measuring. 0 = the
; original uncached run. See the long note inside Main().
#NL_MMU = 1

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; NlMmuOff - hand Anvil back an untranslated machine, from anywhere.
;
; A DEFECT FOUND BY READING, 2026-08-26, WHILE ADDING STAGE 0.6.
;
; Main() turns the MMU on as its FIRST act and turned it off again at
; its LAST line - and it has four exits, not one. MailboxInit failing,
; DisplayInit failing and a surface too small for the layout all
; returned straight to Anvil with translation still enabled.
;
; Anvil knows nothing about page tables. Every address it then touches
; goes through a table this payload built and this payload owns, at
; $600000, inside the low payload window that the next `receive` will
; overwrite. The symptom would not be a clean fault - it would be a
; monitor that works until something happens to be mapped differently,
; which is the worst kind.
;
; It had never been seen because all three of those exits are failure
; paths, and on a bench where the screen comes up they never run. That
; is exactly the argument for fixing it rather than noting it: the
; paths that only execute when something else has already gone wrong
; are the ones nobody has watched.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure NlMmuOff()
  If #NL_MMU = 1
    If MmuEnabled() = 1
      MmuDisable()
      UartWriteStr("MMU: off again - Anvil gets an untranslated machine back.")
      PrintNl()
      UartDrain()
    EndIf
  EndIf
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define rc.i
  Define w.i
  Define h.i
  Define pitch.i
  Define bpp.i
  Define order.i
  Define hz.i
  Define pixels.i
  Define probeRows.i
  Define probeUs.i
  Define estUs.i
  Define fullUs.i
  Define clearUs.i
  Define readUs.i
  Define plainUs.i
  Define fusedUs.i
  Define flushUs.i
  Define bestUs.i
  Define src.i
  Define t0.i
  ; --- stage 0.6, the damage-limited frames -------------------------
  Define fullPixels.i
  Define fullFills.i
  Define caretUs.i
  Define caretPx.i
  Define hoverUs.i
  Define hoverPx.i
  Define scrollUs.i
  Define scrollPx.i
  Define mixedUs.i
  Define mixedPx.i
  ; --- the shadow-buffer experiment ---------------------------------
  Define fbUs.i
  Define dramUs.i
  Define copyUs.i
  Define shadowRows.i
  ; --- 9e, the identity check ---------------------------------------
  Define snapUs.i
  Define idHoverUs.i
  Define diffCount.i

  UartInit(115200)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THE CACHES, AND THE BEFORE-AND-AFTER THIS PROGRAM EXISTS FOR
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  #NL_MMU = 0 reproduces the 2026-08-26 measurement exactly: MMU off,
  ;  nothing cached, blend 2713 ms against a 25 ms budget.
  ;  #NL_MMU = 1 builds the identity map and turns translation on with
  ;  DRAM mapped Normal Write-Back Cacheable, then runs the identical
  ;  passes. Which attribute DRAM gets is #MMU_DRAM_ATTR in mmu.pi4.
  ;
  ;  THE MMU IS TURNED OFF AGAIN BEFORE THIS RETURNS. Anvil is still
  ;  running underneath and knows nothing about page tables; handing it
  ;  back a translated address space would be a fault it has no vector
  ;  for. Stage 1 proved the off-again path on this board - SCTLR_EL2
  ;  went $30C50830 -> $30C50831 -> $30C50830 with the console alive.
  ;
  ;  THE TABLES GO AT $600000, inside the low payload window and clear of
  ;  this image at $400000 and its stack at $3000000. 8 KiB, two tables.
  If #NL_MMU = 1
    UartWriteStr("MMU: building the identity map at $600000 ...")
    PrintNl()
    ; MmuBuildTables RETURNS 0 ON SUCCESS and a negative #MMU_ERR_* on
    ; refusal (mmu.pi4:1044-1046, :1144). Testing it as though non-zero
    ; meant success silently ran the whole measurement UNCACHED and
    ; reported the uncached numbers as though they were the cached ones -
    ; which is worse than a crash, because the output looked complete.
    ; pi4MmuIdentity.pi4:216 gets this right with `If rc <> 0`.
    If MmuBuildTables($600000) <> 0
      UartWriteStr("MMU: the tables would not build - running UNCACHED.")
      PrintNl()
    Else
      MmuEnable()
      If MmuEnabled() = 1
        UartWriteStr("MMU: ON. DRAM is Normal Write-Back Cacheable. Every")
        PrintNl()
        UartWriteStr("     number below is the CACHED half of the comparison.")
        PrintNl()
      Else
        UartWriteStr("MMU: the enable did not take - running UNCACHED.")
        PrintNl()
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Else
    UartWriteStr("MMU: off. These are the UNCACHED numbers.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; THIS PAYLOAD DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ARM A WATCHDOG, and the removed
  ; line that used to sit here reset the board after every single run.
  ;
  ; It said SafetyWatchdogStart(30), citing the vault's "Bench practice -
  ; arm the deadman". That rule was misread. Arming is the OPERATOR's
  ; job - `deadman <secs>` at the prompt, then `run` - and the reason is
  ; the half nobody thinks about: ANVIL STOPS IT AGAIN when the payload
  ; returns. A payload that arms its own watchdog leaves one running that
  ; Anvil does not know exists and therefore never stops.
  ;
  ; MEASURED, on 2026-08-26, three runs identically: the payload returned
  ; at 11.0 s, Anvil printed its "returned" line, and the board reset
  ; 14.1 s later. It happened with the screen on and with the screen off,
  ; which is how the HDMI console was cleared of suspicion. Every
  ; screenshot of this program's own picture was truncated by it, because
  ; a 1920x1080 capture over 115200 baud takes minutes and the board was
  ; gone within seconds.
  ;
  ; AND IT ASKED FOR 30 SECONDS AND WAS GIVEN 16. The BCM2711 counter is
  ; 20 bits at 65536/s, so ~15.999 s is the ceiling; safety.pi4:491-498
  ; clamps and RETURNS THE REAL VALUE, and this call site threw that
  ; return away. The library was honest and the caller did not listen -
  ; which is why the passes below print their own elapsed times rather
  ; than trusting any timeout to bound them.
  ;
  ; This program bounds itself instead: every pass is probed over eight
  ; rows first and the full pass runs only if the estimate is survivable.
  ; If you want a net underneath that, type `deadman 15` before `run` -
  ; the whole program measures about 11 s, so 15 fits, and Anvil will
  ; stop it for you afterwards.

  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("=== pi4NeonLiar - stage 0 (the picture) and stage 0.5 (the wall) ===")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("Two things on the screen are deliberately wrong. See the file header:")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("  LIE 1  every glow and every hover is FULLY OPAQUE - display.pi4 has")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("         no blend, so Neon_Glow's four 2-7% boxes land at full strength.")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("  LIE 2  every glyph is 8 pixels wide - the built-in font is fixed width,")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("         the engine's is proportional and antialiased.")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("Neither is a defect. Do not report them.")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ---- 1. bring the surface up ---------------------------------------
  UartWriteStr("-- 1. the surface")
  PrintNl()

  If MailboxInit() = 0
    UartWriteStr("!! MailboxInit failed, MailboxError ")
    PrintDec(MailboxError())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("   Nothing below this line can run. This is a transport failure,")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("   not a graphics one.")
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
    NlMmuOff()
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  DisplayStandardTags()
  rc = DisplayInit(1920, 1080, 32)
  If DisplayReady() = 0
    UartWriteStr("!! DisplayInit returned ")
    PrintDec(rc)
    UartWriteStr(", DisplayLastError ")
    PrintDec(DisplayLastError())
    UartWriteStr(", MailboxError ")
    PrintDec(MailboxError())
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("   display.pi4:916-957 names every one of those codes. The usual")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("   cause is no HDMI display connected when the board powered on -")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("   the firmware only looks for one at boot.")
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
    NlMmuOff()
    ProcedureReturn 2
  EndIf

  ; RED AND BLUE, ASKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED - anvil.pi4:3211-3215 does
  ; exactly this and its comment explains why the answer depends on how
  ; the board booted. Tag $00040006 is GET_PIXEL_ORDER; 0 is BGR and 1
  ; is RGB, from Datasheets/pi4/uboot/v2025.01_mbox.h:373-374.
  order = -1
  If MailboxBegin($00040006, 4, 4) <> 0
    MailboxSetWord(0, 0)
    If MailboxSend() <> 0
      order = MailboxWord(0)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If order = 1
    DisplayColourSwap(1)
  Else
    DisplayColourSwap(0)
  EndIf

  w     = DisplayWidth()
  h     = DisplayHeight()
  pitch = DisplayPitch()
  bpp   = DisplayBytesPerPixel()
  pixels = w * h

  PutLabel("geometry           ")
  PrintDec(w)
  UartWriteStr(" x ")
  PrintDec(h)
  UartWriteStr(" x ")
  PrintDec(DisplayDepth())
  UartWriteStr(" bpp, pitch ")
  PrintDec(pitch)
  PrintNl()

  PutLabel("framebuffer base   $")
  PutHexN(DisplayBase(), 16)
  UartWriteStr("   (compare Anvil's own - if they differ, the")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                    second DisplayInit moved it and a screenshot will")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                    show Anvil's console instead of this picture)")
  PrintNl()

  PutLabel("pixel order        ")
  PrintDec(order)
  UartWriteStr("   (0 = BGR, no swap wanted; 1 = RGB, swap applied; -1 = the")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                    firmware would not say)")
  PrintNl()

  ; THE CLOCK IS ASKED FOR. The part boots at 600 MHz and reaches 1500
  ; only when something raises it, so every cycles-per-byte figure below
  ; would be wrong by a factor of 2.5 if the number were assumed. The
  ; MEASURED rate is the honest one - it differs from the configured
  ; rate whenever the governor has dropped the core.
  hz = MailboxClockRateMeasured(3)
  If hz < 1
    hz = MailboxClockRate(3)
  EndIf
  PutLabel("ARM clock          ")
  PrintDec(hz)
  UartWriteStr(" Hz, measured through the mailbox, not assumed")
  PrintNl()

  If bpp <> 4
    UartWriteStr("!! this surface is not 32bpp. The blend passes below read and write")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("   32-bit pixels directly and would corrupt a 16bpp surface, so they")
    PrintNl()
    UartWriteStr("   are being skipped. The picture will still be drawn.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; AUTO-FLUSH OFF for everything that follows. display.pi4:463-464
  ; documents this exact escape. The flush is then measured on its own
  ; and the surface is flushed once at the very end.
  DisplayAutoFlush(0)

  ; THE PALETTE IS BUILT HERE, not just before the picture, and the
  ; reason is a bug that was in this file for an hour: pass 1 clears the
  ; surface to cBg, and cBg was still zero at that point, so the
  ; fill-rate pass painted the screen BLACK while a comment two lines
  ; away said it was clearing to the Neon ground. The timing would have
  ; been right and the sentence describing it wrong, which is the worst
  ; combination. DisplayRGB is trustworthy from the moment the colour
  ; order is settled, which is the line above, so this belongs here.
  NlPalette()

  src = DisplayRGB(90, 189, 208)             ; the accent, as a wash

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  2. THE FOUR PASSES
  ;
  ;  Each is probed over eight rows first and the full-surface cost
  ;  extrapolated, because nothing on this disk has ever measured
  ;  uncached memory on this board and a diagnostic that disappears
  ;  into the deadman having printed nothing is worth nothing.
  ; ==================================================================
  probeRows = 8

  UartWriteStr("-- 2. STAGE 0.5, pass 1 of 4: DisplayClear, opaque stores only")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("through DspRowFill32's unrolled wide-store loop, display.pi4:2414-2455")
  PrintNl()
  probeUs = NlPassClear(probeRows, cBg)
  estUs = (probeUs * h) / probeRows
  PutLabel("probe, 8 rows      ")
  PrintDec(probeUs)
  UartWriteStr(" us  -> full surface estimated at ")
  PutMs(estUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
  clearUs = 0
  If estUs < 5000000
    clearUs = NlPassClear(h, cBg)
    NlReport(clearUs, pixels, pixels * 4, hz)
  Else
    PutLabel("FULL PASS SKIPPED - the estimate above is over five seconds. The")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("probe is the answer; treat the estimate as the number.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  UartWriteStr("-- 3. STAGE 0.5, pass 2 of 4: read every pixel, write nothing")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("not in the plan. It is here because it is the only way to tell a")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("read-bound wall from a write-bound one, and the blend does both.")
  PrintNl()
  probeUs = NlPassRead(probeRows)
  estUs = (probeUs * h) / probeRows
  PutLabel("probe, 8 rows      ")
  PrintDec(probeUs)
  UartWriteStr(" us  -> full surface estimated at ")
  PutMs(estUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()
  readUs = 0
  If estUs < 5000000
    readUs = NlPassRead(h)
    NlReport(readUs, pixels, pixels * 4, hz)
    PutLabel("checksum           $")
    PutHexN(nlReadAcc, 16)
    UartWriteStr("   (printed so the loop cannot be argued away)")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    PutLabel("FULL PASS SKIPPED - over five seconds estimated.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  plainUs = 0
  fusedUs = 0
  If bpp = 4
    UartWriteStr("-- 4. STAGE 0.5, pass 3 of 4: read-modify-write blend, written to be read")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("dst = (src*a + dst*(255-a)) >> 8 per channel, three locals per pixel")
    PrintNl()
    probeUs = NlPassBlendPlain(probeRows, src, 128)
    estUs = (probeUs * h) / probeRows
    PutLabel("probe, 8 rows      ")
    PrintDec(probeUs)
    UartWriteStr(" us  -> full surface estimated at ")
    PutMs(estUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms")
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
    If estUs < 5000000
      plainUs = NlPassBlendPlain(h, src, 128)
      ; TRAFFIC IS EIGHT BYTES A PIXEL HERE, not four: every pixel is
      ; read and then written.
      NlReport(plainUs, pixels, pixels * 8, hz)
    Else
      PutLabel("FULL PASS SKIPPED - over five seconds estimated.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()

    UartWriteStr("-- 5. STAGE 0.5, pass 4 of 4: the same blend, per-channel locals fused out")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("identical arithmetic. The gap between this and pass 3 is the cost of")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("this backend's statically-allocated locals - display.pi4:2372-2384.")
    PrintNl()
    probeUs = NlPassBlendFused(probeRows, src, 128)
    estUs = (probeUs * h) / probeRows
    PutLabel("probe, 8 rows      ")
    PrintDec(probeUs)
    UartWriteStr(" us  -> full surface estimated at ")
    PutMs(estUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms")
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
    If estUs < 5000000
      fusedUs = NlPassBlendFused(h, src, 128)
      NlReport(fusedUs, pixels, pixels * 8, hz)
    Else
      PutLabel("FULL PASS SKIPPED - over five seconds estimated.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
  EndIf

  ; ---- the fifth number: what the cache maintenance costs -------------
  UartWriteStr("-- 6. the flush, on its own")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("DisplayFlush() is one dc cvac per 64 bytes plus one dsb sy. With the")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("MMU off it is architecturally a no-op, but it still executes. This is")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("the number that decides whether stage 1's GfxFlushRect can default on.")
  PrintNl()
  t0 = Micros()
  DisplayFlush()
  flushUs = Micros() - t0
  PutLabel("elapsed            ")
  PutMs(flushUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms  for ")
  PrintDec((pitch * h) / 64)
  UartWriteStr(" cache lines")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  7. THE VERDICT ON THE 25 ms QUESTION
  ;
  ;  Stated by the program rather than left to be divided out at the
  ;  end of a long session. The BEST of the two blend passes is the one
  ;  that counts: a compositor is allowed to be written well, and the
  ;  question is whether the memory system can carry it, not whether
  ;  the first draft could.
  ; ==================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- 7. the verdict")
  PrintNl()

  ; THE SUMMARY, so the decomposition is on one screen. Four passes over
  ; the same surface: stores only, loads only, then both plus arithmetic
  ; twice. Whichever of these dominates is the thing to attack, and the
  ; whole reason there are four rather than the plan's two.
  PutLabel("pass 1  clear, stores only      ")
  PutMs(clearUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("pass 2  read, loads only        ")
  PutMs(readUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("pass 3  blend, three locals     ")
  PutMs(plainUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("pass 4  blend, locals fused out ")
  PutMs(fusedUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("        whole-surface flush     ")
  PutMs(flushUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("A zero above means that pass was skipped as too slow to run in full;")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("its eight-row probe and extrapolation are printed in its own section.")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; The BEST of the two blend passes is the one the verdict uses: a
  ; compositor is allowed to be written well, and the question is
  ; whether the memory system can carry it, not whether a first draft
  ; could. Start from whichever actually ran - an earlier version
  ; started from plainUs unconditionally and would have reported "no
  ; blend number" in the case where pass 3 was skipped and pass 4 ran.
  bestUs = plainUs
  If bestUs < 1
    bestUs = fusedUs
  EndIf
  If fusedUs > 0 And fusedUs < bestUs
    bestUs = fusedUs
  EndIf

  If bestUs < 1
    PutLabel("NO BLEND NUMBER. Either the surface is not 32bpp or the full pass was")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("skipped as too slow. In the skipped case the estimate above IS the")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("answer and it is far worse than 25 ms, so read it as MMU FIRST.")
    PrintNl()
  ElseIf bestUs > 25000
    PutLabel("OVER BUDGET. The best full-surface blend is ")
    PutMs(bestUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms against a 25 ms")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("budget. A 1920x1080 alpha-composited UI is NOT reachable in this")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("memory configuration WITH THE MMU OFF. Per the plan's stage 0.5,")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("the MMU work moves ahead of everything else - and that is now a")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("SMALL step, not a research project: MMU stage 1 passed on this")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("board on 2026-08-26, SCTLR_EL2 $30C50830 -> $30C50831 -> $30C50830")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("with the console alive throughout. So the next move is to map DRAM")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("Normal Cacheable and RE-RUN THIS EXACT PROGRAM. These numbers are")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("the before half of a before-and-after worth one afternoon.")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("Before concluding it is memory at all, compare passes 3 and 4. If 4")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("is far better than 3, a large part of this is the calling convention")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("and not the DRAM, and the inner loop is the cheaper fix of the two.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    PutLabel("WITHIN BUDGET. The best full-surface blend is ")
    PutMs(bestUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms against a")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("25 ms budget. A full-screen alpha composite is reachable WITH THE")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("MMU OFF, so the plan's stage 1 proceeds as written and the MMU does")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("not have to jump the queue on graphics grounds.")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("Two cautions before celebrating. This is ONE full-surface wash and a")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("real frame has overdraw - GlUIChrome.pbi:323-328 puts four nested")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("boxes behind every hovered surface - so divide the headroom by the")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("overdraw factor. And the 25 ms line is a 1920x1080 line; this")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("surface is smaller after overscan, so scale it before quoting it.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  8. THE PICTURE
  ;
  ;  Last, so that it survives on the panel. The passes above have just
  ;  washed the whole surface and would otherwise erase it.
  ; ==================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- 8. STAGE 0, the picture")
  PrintNl()

  ; A SURFACE TOO SMALL FOR THE LAYOUT IS NO SURFACE, and refusing is
  ; better than drawing a menu bar over a panel over a status bar and
  ; letting somebody wonder which part is the bug. This is the same
  ; refusal, for the same reason, that anvil.pi4:3155-3162 makes before
  ; it will build a console. The stage 0.5 numbers above are unaffected
  ; and stand on their own.
  If w < 800 Or h < 600
    UartWriteStr("!! the surface is ")
    PrintDec(w)
    UartWriteStr(" x ")
    PrintDec(h)
    UartWriteStr(", and this layout needs at least 800 x 600.")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("The picture is being skipped rather than drawn on top of itself.")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("The four passes above are still valid - they do not care about")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("the shape of the surface, only its size in pixels.")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("Sections 9 and 10 go with the picture and are skipped too - both")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("of them repaint this layout and neither means anything without it.")
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
    NlMmuOff()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; THE DAMAGE LAYER IS BOUND TO THE SURFACE, not to the display: it
  ; takes two numbers and knows nothing about a framebuffer. This is
  ; the seam the port plan's section 8.3 asks for, and it is the reason
  ; the same tracker will sit unchanged on top of a DMA blitter.
  If DamageBind(w, h) <> #DMG_OK
    UartWriteStr("!! DamageBind refused with ")
    PrintDec(DamageLastError())
    UartWriteStr(" - the damage sections below are skipped.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf

  ; The full paint, INCLUDING its whole-surface flush, so that it is
  ; the same kind of number as the damaged frames below. The flush is
  ; the escape display.pi4 documents at :463-464 and the reason
  ; auto-flush was turned off at the top rather than left on.
  fullUs = NlPaintFull()
  fullPixels = nlPixels
  fullFills = nlFills

  PutLabel("drawn in           ")
  PutMs(fullUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms, including one whole-surface flush")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("pixels touched     ")
  PrintDec(fullPixels)
  UartWriteStr("   (overdraw: the surface is ")
  PrintDec(pixels)
  UartWriteStr(" px)")
  PrintNl()

  ; THE COUNTERS ARE THE PROOF OF SECTION 1.1. Two primitives and a
  ; scissor, and the numbers say whether that was true of this screen.
  PutLabel("DisplayFillRect    ")
  PrintDec(nlFills)
  UartWriteStr("   <- every box, frame, glow, panel, check mark")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("DisplayTextClear   ")
  PrintDec(nlTexts)
  UartWriteStr("   <- unclipped strings, one call each")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("DisplayCharClear   ")
  PrintDec(nlGlyphs)
  UartWriteStr("   <- clipped strings, one call per glyph")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("any other draw     0   <- there is no third primitive in this file")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  PutLabel("boxes narrowed by the clip   ")
  PrintDec(nlNarrowed)
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("boxes removed by the clip    ")
  PrintDec(nlDropped)
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("glyph cells removed by clip  ")
  PrintDec(nlGlyphsCut)
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("If those three are all zero the scissor proved nothing and the list")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("is not actually overflowing its well. That would be a defect.")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  9. STAGE 0.6 - THE SAME SCREEN, ONE WIDGET AT A TIME
  ;
  ;  The picture above cost a full redraw. A real UI does not redraw a
  ;  screen when a caret blinks, and this section is the measurement of
  ;  how much that is worth on this hardware.
  ;
  ;  EVERY FRAME BELOW WALKS THE WHOLE WIDGET TREE. Nothing is skipped
  ;  at the application level - the menu bar, the list, the panels and
  ;  the status bar are all visited, exactly as they were for the full
  ;  paint. The only difference is that each primitive asks the damage
  ;  list whether it is inside anything that changed. So the numbers
  ;  below include the cost of deciding not to draw, which is the
  ;  honest way to measure this: a driver that knew in advance which
  ;  widgets to visit would be measuring a different design.
  ; ==================================================================
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("-- 9. STAGE 0.6, damage-limited repaints of the same screen")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("the tree is walked in full every time. Only the primitives change.")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ---- 9a. a caret blink --------------------------------------------
  DamageReset()
  NlEventCaret()
  caretUs = NlPaintDamaged()
  caretPx = nlPixels
  PutLabel("9a CARET BLINK     ")
  PutMs(caretUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms   ")
  PrintDec(DamageCount())
  UartWriteStr(" rect, ")
  PrintDec(DamageArea())
  UartWriteStr(" px damaged, ")
  PrintDec(caretPx)
  UartWriteStr(" px painted")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   ")
  PrintDec(nlFills)
  UartWriteStr(" fills and ")
  PrintDec(nlGlyphs)
  UartWriteStr(" glyphs drawn; ")
  PrintDec(nlDmgSkipped)
  UartWriteStr(" primitives rejected by the damage list")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ---- 9b. a hover moving between two buttons -----------------------
  DamageReset()
  NlEventHover(0)
  hoverUs = NlPaintDamaged()
  hoverPx = nlPixels
  PutLabel("9b HOVER MOVES     ")
  PutMs(hoverUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms   ")
  PrintDec(DamageCount())
  UartWriteStr(" rects, ")
  PrintDec(DamageArea())
  UartWriteStr(" px damaged, ")
  PrintDec(hoverPx)
  UartWriteStr(" px painted")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   two rectangles: the button losing the glow and the")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   one gaining it. If this says one, they were merged")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   and the slack is set too high for this layout.")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ---- 9c. a list scrolled by one row -------------------------------
  DamageReset()
  NlEventScroll(1)
  scrollUs = NlPaintDamaged()
  scrollPx = nlPixels
  PutLabel("9c LIST SCROLL     ")
  PutMs(scrollUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms   ")
  PrintDec(DamageCount())
  UartWriteStr(" rect, ")
  PrintDec(DamageArea())
  UartWriteStr(" px damaged, ")
  PrintDec(scrollPx)
  UartWriteStr(" px painted")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   the whole well, because every row moved. This is")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   the WORST case for a damage layer and it is still")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   the honest one - see NlEventScroll on why the")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   scroll-by-copy trick is deliberately not used.")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ---- 9d. all three at once ----------------------------------------
  ; THE BUSIEST FRAME A REAL UI HAS: the pointer moves off one button
  ; and onto another while a caret blinks and a list scrolls under the
  ; wheel. Three unrelated regions. The count is the coalescer's answer
  ; and the area is what it costs.
  DamageReset()
  NlEventCaret()
  NlEventHover(3)
  NlEventScroll(-1)
  mixedUs = NlPaintDamaged()
  mixedPx = nlPixels
  PutLabel("9d ALL THREE       ")
  PutMs(mixedUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms   ")
  PrintDec(DamageCount())
  UartWriteStr(" rects, ")
  PrintDec(DamageArea())
  UartWriteStr(" px damaged, ")
  PrintDec(mixedPx)
  UartWriteStr(" px painted")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   bounding box would be ")
  PrintDec(DamageBoundsW() * DamageBoundsH())
  UartWriteStr(" px - which is what a")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("                   tracker without a cost model would have repainted.")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ---- 9e. the fast frame must be the RIGHT frame -------------------
  ; See the long note above NlSnapshot. This is the check that makes
  ; the numbers above worth quoting.
  If bpp = 4
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("9e IDENTITY CHECK - is the damaged frame the same picture as a")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("   full redraw of the same state? Every pixel is compared.")
    PrintNl()

    ; state B, in full, kept in the shadow as the reference
    nlHoverBtn = 1
    NlPaintFull()
    snapUs = NlSnapshot(#NL_SHADOW, pitch, DisplayBase(), pitch, w, h)

    ; state A, in full, over the top
    nlHoverBtn = 3
    NlPaintFull()

    ; A -> B by the event, damage-limited. Its own variable, because
    ; the ratio section below still needs 9b's number and an earlier
    ; draft quietly overwrote it here - which would have reported the
    ; identity check's timing as though it were the hover frame's.
    DamageReset()
    NlEventHover(1)
    idHoverUs = NlPaintDamaged()

    diffCount = NlDiff(DisplayBase(), pitch, #NL_SHADOW, pitch, w, h)
    PutLabel("   the reference snapshot took ")
    PutMs(snapUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms; the compare is a full read pass")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("   pixels differing from the full redraw   ")
    PrintDec(diffCount)
    PrintNl()
    If diffCount = 0
      PutLabel("   IDENTICAL. The damage-limited repaint produced exactly the")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   picture the full repaint produces, in ")
      PutMs(idHoverUs)
      UartWriteStr(" ms instead of ")
      PutMs(fullUs)
      UartWriteStr(" ms.")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   That is the deliverable, and it is now proven rather than")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   photographed.")
      PrintNl()
    Else
      PutLabel("   ** THE DAMAGED FRAME IS NOT THE FULL FRAME. ** First difference")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   at x ")
      PrintDec(nlDiffX)
      UartWriteStr(", y ")
      PrintDec(nlDiffY)
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   Some event under-reported what it would repaint. A small count")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   means an edge - most likely Neon_Glow's four-pixel ring, which")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   NlDamageButton exists to include. A large count means a whole")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   widget was missed. DO NOT QUOTE THE TIMINGS ABOVE until this")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("   reads zero: they are the cost of drawing the wrong picture.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
    PrintNl()
    UartDrain()
  EndIf

  ; ---- the ratio, stated -------------------------------------------
  PutLabel("full repaint       ")
  PutMs(fullUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms, ")
  PrintDec(fullPixels)
  UartWriteStr(" px painted, ")
  PrintDec(fullFills)
  UartWriteStr(" fills")
  PrintNl()
  If caretUs > 0
    PutLabel("caret is faster by ")
    PrintDec(fullUs / caretUs)
    UartWriteStr("x")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    PutLabel("the caret frame was too fast for the microsecond counter to see,")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("which is itself the answer: it is under one microsecond.")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  If hoverUs > 0
    PutLabel("hover is faster by ")
    PrintDec(fullUs / hoverUs)
    UartWriteStr("x")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  If scrollUs > 0
    PutLabel("scroll is faster by ")
    PrintDec(fullUs / scrollUs)
    UartWriteStr("x")
    PrintNl()
  EndIf
  PutLabel("THE FLOOR IS THE TRAVERSAL, NOT THE PIXELS. A frame that paints")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("almost nothing still walks the tree and still asks the damage list")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("about every primitive. Whatever the caret frame costs above is")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("very nearly that floor, and no smaller change can beat it. If it")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("is a large fraction of the frame budget, the next move is a")
  PrintNl()
  PutLabel("retained widget tree, NOT a better damage rule.")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  10. THE SHADOW-BUFFER QUESTION
  ;
  ;  One pair of numbers decides it. See the long note above
  ;  NlPassStore for why this is the experiment and not another.
  ; ==================================================================
  UartWriteStr("-- 10. shadow buffer, or straight into the live framebuffer?")
  PrintNl()

  If bpp <> 4
    PutLabel("skipped - this surface is not 32bpp.")
    PrintNl()
  Else
    ; A quarter of the surface, so the whole section is fast even if
    ; the framebuffer turns out to be as slow as stage 0.5 says. The
    ; two destinations get IDENTICAL geometry, which is the only thing
    ; that makes them comparable.
    shadowRows = h / 4
    If shadowRows < 1
      shadowRows = 1
    EndIf

    fbUs   = NlPassStore(DisplayBase(), pitch, w, shadowRows, cBg)
    dramUs = NlPassStore(#NL_SHADOW,    pitch, w, shadowRows, cBg)

    PutLabel("the SAME store loop, ")
    PrintDec(shadowRows)
    UartWriteStr(" rows of ")
    PrintDec(w)
    UartWriteStr(" pixels, two destinations:")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("  into the framebuffer   ")
    PutMs(fbUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms")
    PrintNl()
    PutLabel("  into plain DRAM        ")
    PutMs(dramUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms   ($01000000, inside Anvil's low payload window)")
    PrintNl()

    If dramUs > 0
      PutLabel("  ratio                 ")
      PutFix2((fbUs * 100) / dramUs)
      UartWriteStr("x")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf

    ; The presentation step: one damaged rectangle out of the shadow
    ; and into the framebuffer. Sized as the hover frame's damage,
    ; because that is the rectangle a real UI would actually be
    ; copying.
    copyUs = NlPassCopy(DisplayBase(), pitch, #NL_SHADOW, pitch, 0, 0, nlBtnW + 8, nlBtnH + 8)
    PutLabel("  a hover-sized damaged copy, ")
    PrintDec(nlBtnW + 8)
    UartWriteStr(" x ")
    PrintDec(nlBtnH + 8)
    UartWriteStr(": ")
    PutMs(copyUs)
    UartWriteStr(" ms")
    PrintNl()
    PrintNl()

    ; THE VERDICT, COMPUTED. The threshold is 2x and the reasoning is
    ; arithmetic rather than taste: a shadow strategy writes every
    ; pixel once into DRAM and then copies it once into the
    ; framebuffer, so it pays one DRAM write plus one DRAM read plus
    ; one framebuffer write against the direct strategy's one
    ; framebuffer write. It can only win if a framebuffer write is
    ; expensive enough to dominate all three, and the crossover is
    ; around twice - below that the extra traffic eats the saving.
    If dramUs < 1
      PutLabel("VERDICT: the DRAM pass was too fast to time at this size. Re-run")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("with more rows before drawing a conclusion from it.")
      PrintNl()
    ElseIf fbUs > (dramUs * 2)
      PutLabel("VERDICT: SHADOW-BUFFER. A store into the framebuffer costs more")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("than twice what the identical store into DRAM costs, so the")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("framebuffer - not the compositor and not the arithmetic - is the")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("wall. Compose into the shadow at DRAM speed and copy out only")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("the damaged rectangles. Note what makes that affordable: the")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("copy is proportional to the DAMAGE, so the shadow buffer and the")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("damage layer are one decision, not two.")
      PrintNl()
    Else
      PutLabel("VERDICT: NO SHADOW BUFFER. The framebuffer is not meaningfully")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("slower than DRAM, so a shadow would add a second write and a")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("read to save nothing. Repaint the damage straight into the live")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("framebuffer.")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("AND TEARING IS NOT A REASON TO RECONSIDER AT THESE RATES. A")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("frame that repaints a caret in microseconds cannot be caught")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("half-drawn by a 60 Hz scan-out in any way a person could see;")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("the full repaint CAN be, and takes hundreds of milliseconds, so")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("it would tear with or without a shadow. Tearing becomes a real")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("question only once a frame is both large and fast, which is")
      PrintNl()
      PutLabel("exactly the state this hardware is not in.")
      PrintNl()
    EndIf
  EndIf
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; The screen is left holding the damaged frames' state, which is a
  ; slightly different picture from the one described in the header -
  ; button 3 hovered again, the caret blinked twice, the list scrolled
  ; down and back. Section 10 then washed the top quarter of it with
  ; the ground colour. So the panel is repainted one last time, in
  ; full, and flushed, and what the camera sees is the stage 0 picture
  ; the header describes.
  ;
  ; MEASURING FIRST AND DRAWING LAST IS THE HOUSE ORDER HERE and it is
  ; the reason this line exists rather than a note apologising for a
  ; quarter-blanked screen.
  fullUs = NlPaintFull()
  PutLabel("the panel repainted in full one last time, ")
  PutMs(fullUs)
  UartWriteStr(" ms")
  PrintNl()
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  UartWriteStr("Look at the panel now. Anvil is about to print two or three lines of")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("its own over the top of the picture - that is Anvil, not this program.")
  PrintNl()
  UartWriteStr("Then: python tools/pmfshot.py COM7 liar.png")
  PrintNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; Hand Anvil back the address space it thinks it has. The same call
  ; now guards the three failure exits above - see NlMmuOff.
  NlMmuOff()

  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
